/* Authored source: physics-focused-authoring.txt and physics-extra-authoring.json. */
window.PhysicsFocusedContent={"version":"2026.10.12-depth","specs":{"y11-foundations":[{"title":"SI units and scientific notation","start":3,"theory":"A measured quantity needs both a number and a unit. Prefixes are scale factors, not additional physical quantities. Convert the entire measurement before substituting into an equation; a squared or cubed unit also squares or cubes its conversion factor. Scientific notation makes these factors visible. Keep calculator guard digits until the final reported answer.","question":"Convert 72.0 km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹ and explain why multiplying by 1000 alone is insufficient.","points":["72.0 km h⁻¹ = 72.0 × 1000/3600 m s⁻¹","The result is 20.0 m s⁻¹ to three significant figures","Both the distance unit and the time unit must be converted"],"claim":"Converting km h⁻¹ to m s⁻¹ only requires multiplication by 1000."},{"title":"Rearranging equations and checking dimensions","start":12,"theory":"An equation represents a relationship, so an operation performed on one side must also be performed on the other. Isolate the unknown symbol before inserting numbers. A dimensional check cannot prove that an equation is correct, but it can expose impossible combinations. Energy divided by time, for example, has the dimensions of power, whereas energy multiplied by time does not.","question":"An object has kinetic energy 90.0 J and mass 5.00 kg. Rearrange Ek = ½mv² to find its speed.","points":["v² = 2Ek/m, so v = √(2Ek/m)","v = √(2 × 90.0/5.00) = 6.00 m s⁻¹","Taking the square root is essential; 36.0 is v² rather than v"],"claim":"Solving Ek = ½mv² for speed gives v = 2Ek/m."},{"title":"Measurement, uncertainty and best-fit gradients","start":16,"theory":"Instrument resolution, scatter and calibration offsets describe different limitations. Repeating readings helps estimate random scatter, but averaging does not cancel a consistent zero error. State an absolute uncertainty in the same units as the value. For a gradient, choose widely separated points on the best-fit line rather than two convenient experimental dots, and derive its units from the axes.","question":"A length is (0.500 ± 0.005) m and a time is (2.00 ± 0.04) s. Find speed and percentage uncertainty using added relative uncertainties.","points":["v = 0.500/2.00 = 0.250 m s⁻¹","Percentage uncertainty = 1.0% + 2.0% = 3.0%","Absolute uncertainty = 0.0075 m s⁻¹, approximately 0.008 m s⁻¹"],"claim":"Repeating a measurement automatically removes a systematic zero error."}],"y11-heat-temperature":[{"title":"Particle motion and internal energy","start":3,"theory":"Particles in a solid still move: they vibrate around relatively fixed positions. Liquid particles remain close but rearrange; gas particles are widely separated and move between collisions. Internal energy includes microscopic kinetic energy and energy associated with particle interactions. It does not mean the kinetic energy of the whole container moving across the room. Comparing total internal energy requires attention to substance, amount and state.","question":"Compare two samples of liquid water at 25 °C, one 100 g and one 500 g. Discuss average particle kinetic energy and total internal energy.","points":["The same temperature corresponds to the same average particle kinetic energy in this comparison","The larger sample contains more particles","For the same substance and state, the larger sample has greater total internal energy"],"claim":"Particles in a solid have no kinetic energy because the solid keeps its shape."},{"title":"Heat, temperature and thermal equilibrium","start":9,"theory":"Temperature determines the direction of net spontaneous energy transfer, rather than measuring the total energy in a body. Heat is energy in transit because of a temperature difference; it is not a substance stored in an object. At thermal equilibrium microscopic exchanges can continue, but there is no net transfer between the bodies. Equal temperatures do not require equal masses or equal internal energies.","question":"A small metal spoon at 70 °C touches a large container of water at 20 °C. Explain the initial energy transfer and what thermal equilibrium means.","points":["Net energy initially transfers from the hotter spoon to the cooler water","The direction is determined by temperature rather than sample size","At equilibrium they have the same temperature and no net energy transfer between them"],"claim":"The larger object always transfers heat to the smaller object."},{"title":"Kelvin scales and interpreting thermometer data","start":13,"theory":"Kelvin is an absolute temperature scale: T/K = temperature/°C + 273.15. A temperature difference has the same numerical size in kelvin and degrees Celsius because the intervals are equal. An absolute value and a difference must not be confused. A thermometer also takes time to reach equilibrium, so reading immediately after immersion can introduce a systematic lag.","question":"Water warms from 18.0 °C to 33.0 °C. Give both temperatures in kelvin and the temperature change in kelvin.","points":["Initial temperature = 291.15 K and final temperature = 306.15 K","The change is 33.0 − 18.0 = 15.0 K","Do not add 273.15 to a temperature difference"],"claim":"A rise of 15 °C is a rise of 288.15 K."}],"y11-specific-latent":[{"title":"Specific heat capacity and proportional reasoning","start":3,"theory":"Specific heat capacity describes the energy needed per kilogram per kelvin of temperature change while the substance stays in one phase. In Q = mcΔT, the mass is in kilograms and ΔT is a difference, not a final temperature. A high heat capacity produces a smaller temperature rise for the same energy and mass. Cooling gives a negative energy change for the cooling body when ΔT is signed.","question":"A 0.250 kg metal block with c = 900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ warms by 12.0 K. Find the energy absorbed and predict the rise for twice the mass with the same energy.","points":["Q = mcΔT = 0.250 × 900 × 12.0 = 2700 J","For fixed Q and c, ΔT is inversely proportional to mass","Twice the mass rises by 6.00 K"],"claim":"Doubling the mass doubles the temperature rise for the same heating energy."},{"title":"Latent heat and particle potential energy","start":11,"theory":"During a phase change at constant pressure, energy changes particle arrangement and interactions rather than raising average particle kinetic energy. A plateau therefore does not mean that the heater has stopped transferring energy. Use Q = mL for the mass actually changing phase. Fusion and vaporisation have different latent heats; melting is not the same energy process as warming liquid water.","question":"At its melting point, 0.0400 kg of ice melts completely. Use Lf = 3.34 × 10⁵ J kg⁻¹. Explain the constant temperature.","points":["Q = mLf = 0.0400 × 3.34 × 10⁵ = 1.336 × 10⁴ J","Energy changes particle interactions and arrangement during melting","Average particle kinetic energy and hence temperature remain constant during the ideal phase change"],"claim":"A flat region on a heating curve proves that no energy enters the sample."},{"title":"Heating curves and multi-stage energy budgets","start":15,"theory":"Separate a heating journey at every phase boundary. Sloping sections use a heat-capacity equation; plateaux use a latent-heat equation. The mass usually stays constant, but the material's specific heat capacity changes between phases. Add each stage's energy before calculating heater time. If a heater loses energy to the surroundings, its electrical input is greater than the sample's energy gain.","question":"Find the energy to melt 0.0200 kg of ice at 0 °C and then warm the water to 10.0 °C. Use Lf = 334000 J kg⁻¹ and cw = 4180 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.","points":["Melting requires 0.0200 × 334000 = 6680 J","Warming requires 0.0200 × 4180 × 10.0 = 836 J","Total energy absorbed is 7516 J, approximately 7.52 kJ"],"claim":"A single mcΔT calculation includes the energy needed to change phase."}],"y11-transfer-calorimetry":[{"title":"Conduction, convection and thermal radiation","start":3,"theory":"Conduction transfers energy through interactions between neighbouring particles, including mobile electrons in metals. Convection transfers energy with the bulk motion of a fluid. Radiation is transfer by electromagnetic waves and can occur through a vacuum. A real system may use all three mechanisms simultaneously. An explanation should identify the physical process in the stated location, rather than merely naming a mechanism.","question":"Explain why a vacuum flask has a vacuum gap and reflective surfaces. State one remaining path for energy transfer.","points":["The vacuum gap largely suppresses conduction and convection between the walls","Reflective surfaces reduce radiative transfer","Energy can still transfer through the stopper or neck by conduction"],"claim":"A vacuum stops thermal radiation because radiation requires particles."},{"title":"Thermal equilibrium and calorimetry equations","start":10,"theory":"Define the system before writing an energy balance. In an insulated mixture, energy lost by warmer components equals energy gained by cooler components. Each term needs its own mass and temperature change. The final temperature is shared by all components at equilibrium. A room-temperature calorimeter may also absorb energy; neglecting its heat capacity is a modelling assumption that should be stated.","question":"Mix 0.100 kg water at 60.0 °C with 0.300 kg water at 20.0 °C in an insulated vessel of negligible heat capacity. Find equilibrium temperature.","points":["0.100c(60.0 − T) = 0.300c(T − 20.0)","6.00 − 0.100T = 0.300T − 6.00 gives T = 30.0 °C","The temperature is closer to that of the larger cold-water mass"],"claim":"The final temperature of two water samples is always their simple arithmetic mean."},{"title":"Phase-change mixtures, efficiency and cooling systems","start":16,"theory":"When ice is added to warm water, test whether enough energy is available to melt all of it before assuming a final temperature above zero. Energy used for melting is unavailable for subsequent warming. Efficiency compares useful output to total input for a stated device and purpose. A refrigerator transfers energy from a cold region to a warmer one by using work; it does not destroy thermal energy.","question":"A refrigerator removes 300 J from its interior while receiving 100 J of electrical work. Find the energy delivered to the room and explain why this conserves energy.","points":["Energy leaving the refrigerator system is 300 + 100 = 400 J","The room receives both the removed thermal energy and the work input","The electrical input enables transfer from the colder interior to the warmer room"],"claim":"A refrigerator eliminates heat rather than transferring energy to its surroundings."}],"y11-vectors":[{"title":"Scalars, displacement and vector diagrams","start":3,"theory":"A scalar needs magnitude; a vector also needs direction. Choose an origin and positive axis before using signs. Distance accumulates along the route, whereas displacement compares only initial and final positions. In a head-to-tail diagram the resultant joins the original tail to the final head. Arrow lengths need a scale if the diagram is used to estimate a numerical magnitude.","question":"A walker travels 80 m east and 30 m west in 50 s. Find distance, displacement and average velocity.","points":["Distance is 80 + 30 = 110 m","With east positive, displacement is 80 − 30 = 50 m east","Average velocity is 50/50 = 1.0 m s⁻¹ east"],"claim":"Distance and displacement have identical values for every journey."},{"title":"Components and vector addition or subtraction","start":10,"theory":"Components are projections of one vector onto chosen perpendicular axes, not extra forces or displacements. The component adjacent to a stated angle uses cosine. First add signed components, then reconstruct magnitude and direction. Vector subtraction reverses the subtracted vector. A change in velocity can be large even when the initial and final speed magnitudes are similar because their directions matter.","question":"A force of 40.0 N acts 60.0° above the horizontal. Find its components and explain whether the components should be added to the original force.","points":["Fx = 40.0 cos60.0° = 20.0 N","Fy = 40.0 sin60.0° = 34.6 N","The components represent the same force and must not be counted again alongside it"],"claim":"Resolving a force creates two additional independent forces."},{"title":"Average motion and vector interpretation","start":15,"theory":"Average velocity divides the net displacement by the complete elapsed time, including pauses. Average speed divides the total path length by the same time. Neither necessarily describes the motion at any particular instant. A round trip can have zero average velocity and a nonzero average speed. State direction in a velocity answer and check that the chosen averaging interval matches the question.","question":"A cyclist rides 300 m north in 60 s, pauses for 20 s, then rides 300 m south in 40 s. Calculate both averages.","points":["Total time is 120 s including the pause","Average speed is 600/120 = 5.00 m s⁻¹","Net displacement is zero so average velocity is zero"],"claim":"A stationary pause can be omitted from the total time when calculating the journey's average speed."}],"y11-motion-graphs":[{"title":"Position graphs, gradients and tangents","start":3,"theory":"Position is a coordinate, so a negative position does not imply motion in the negative direction. On a position–time graph, gradient determines velocity. A straight line means constant velocity; a curve requires a tangent to find instantaneous velocity. When estimating a tangent gradient, use two points far apart on the tangent itself. Its units come from position divided by time.","question":"A straight position–time segment goes from (2.0 s, 12 m) to (6.0 s, 4.0 m). Find velocity and explain the direction.","points":["Gradient = (4.0 − 12)/(6.0 − 2.0)","Velocity = −2.0 m s⁻¹","The position decreases with time, so motion is in the negative direction"],"claim":"A point below the position axis always means the object is moving backwards."},{"title":"Velocity graphs, signed areas and reversals","start":8,"theory":"A velocity–time gradient gives acceleration and a signed area gives displacement. A segment below the time axis indicates negative velocity; it does not automatically mean slowing down. Split area calculations where velocity crosses zero. Adding absolute areas gives distance, while algebraic addition gives displacement. This distinction is essential for journeys with reversals.","question":"An object has velocity +3.0 m s⁻¹ for 4.0 s, then −2.0 m s⁻¹ for 3.0 s. Find distance and displacement.","points":["Positive area is +12 m and negative area is −6.0 m","Displacement is +6.0 m","Distance is 12 + 6.0 = 18 m"],"claim":"Every area under a velocity–time graph contributes positively to displacement."},{"title":"Acceleration graphs and linking representations","start":13,"theory":"Area under an acceleration–time graph gives change in velocity, rather than displacement. Recover final velocity by adding this change to the initial velocity. The three motion graphs must describe the same events: a stationary point of position corresponds to zero velocity, while a flat velocity segment corresponds to zero acceleration. Use the axis labels to decide the meaning before calculating a gradient or area.","question":"Acceleration is −1.5 m s⁻² for 4.0 s from an initial velocity of +8.0 m s⁻¹. Find the velocity change and final velocity.","points":["Δv = aΔt = −1.5 × 4.0 = −6.0 m s⁻¹","v = 8.0 − 6.0 = +2.0 m s⁻¹","The object slows without reversing because final velocity remains positive"],"claim":"Area under an acceleration–time graph directly gives the object's displacement."}],"y11-kinematics":[{"title":"Choosing and rearranging constant-acceleration equations","start":3,"theory":"List u, v, a, s and t with signs and units. An equation is useful when it includes the requested unknown and the available quantities, without introducing another unnecessary unknown. The constant-acceleration assumption applies separately to each stage. If acceleration changes, split the motion. Dimensional and magnitude checks are a final safeguard against an inappropriate equation or calculator entry.","question":"A cart accelerates uniformly from 2.00 to 8.00 m s⁻¹ over 15.0 m. Calculate acceleration without finding time first.","points":["Use v² = u² + 2as because time is not needed","a = (8.00² − 2.00²)/(2 × 15.0)","a = 2.00 m s⁻² in the positive direction"],"claim":"A constant-acceleration calculation always requires time as an input."},{"title":"Free fall and upward throws","start":10,"theory":"With upward positive, gravitational acceleration remains negative during both ascent and descent. At the highest point velocity is zero only momentarily; acceleration is still downward. Treating the entire flight as one constant-acceleration motion is often simpler than splitting it. A return to launch height has zero net displacement but a nonzero path length. A launch from a platform needs the ground's actual displacement coordinate.","question":"A ball is thrown upward at 14.7 m s⁻¹. With g = 9.80 m s⁻², find time to the highest point and maximum rise.","points":["At the highest point v = 0, while a = −9.80 m s⁻²","t = (0 − 14.7)/(−9.80) = 1.50 s","Maximum rise = u²/(2g) = 11.0 m"],"claim":"A ball's acceleration is zero at the highest point because its velocity is zero."},{"title":"Reaction distance, braking and model limits","start":15,"theory":"Stopping distance combines a constant-speed reaction stage and a decelerating braking stage. A driver cannot brake before responding to the hazard. Reaction distance grows directly with initial speed for a fixed reaction time. Braking distance grows with speed squared for a fixed deceleration magnitude. Wet roads, tyre condition and slope can alter the assumptions, so the ideal calculation is a model rather than a universal stopping guarantee.","question":"At 15.0 m s⁻¹ a driver reacts for 0.800 s then brakes at 5.00 m s⁻². Calculate total stopping distance.","points":["Reaction distance = 15.0 × 0.800 = 12.0 m","Braking distance = 15.0²/(2 × 5.00) = 22.5 m","Total stopping distance = 34.5 m"],"claim":"Braking distance doubles whenever initial speed doubles at the same deceleration."}],"y11-newton":[{"title":"Free-body diagrams, inertia and balanced forces","start":3,"theory":"Isolate one object and show only forces exerted on that object. A force arrow should identify its source and direction; motion arrows belong on a separate diagram. Zero resultant force means zero acceleration, allowing either rest or constant velocity. Mass measures inertia, the resistance to a change in velocity. It does not describe a force, and an object does not need a forward resultant to keep moving uniformly.","question":"A trolley moves right at constant velocity on a horizontal track. A 6.0 N pull acts right. State the horizontal resisting force and explain the resultant.","points":["Constant velocity means acceleration is zero","Newton's second law therefore requires zero horizontal resultant","The horizontal resisting force is 6.0 N left"],"claim":"A moving object must always have a resultant force in its direction of motion."},{"title":"Resultant force, mass and Newton's third law","start":8,"theory":"Newton's second law applies to the vector sum of forces on one chosen object. Newton's third law compares a pair of interactions acting on different objects. Their equal magnitudes do not make either individual object force-free. A truck and a car can exert equal collision forces yet experience different accelerations because their masses differ. Keep the system boundary explicit when considering whether forces are internal or external.","question":"A 2.00 kg trolley is pulled right with 9.0 N while friction is 3.0 N left. Find acceleration and identify the reaction to the pull.","points":["Net horizontal force is 9.0 − 3.0 = 6.0 N right","Acceleration is 6.0/2.00 = 3.0 m s⁻² right","The trolley pulls left on the pulling object with 9.0 N"],"claim":"The force of the pull and friction on the trolley are a Newton's third-law pair."},{"title":"Apparent weight and accelerating lifts","start":15,"theory":"A scale measures the normal contact force, not gravitational force directly. Choose the person's free-body diagram and a vertical sign convention. With upward positive, N − mg = ma. A descending lift may accelerate upward while slowing, so its motion direction alone does not determine the reading. In free fall the normal force can be zero even though gravity still acts.","question":"A 50.0 kg person descends while slowing with upward acceleration 2.00 m s⁻². Find the scale reading using g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["Upward acceleration is +2.00 m s⁻² despite downward velocity","N = m(g + a) = 50.0(9.80 + 2.00)","The scale reads 590 N, greater than the 490 N weight"],"claim":"A descending lift always gives a scale reading smaller than the person's weight."}],"y11-friction-slopes":[{"title":"Static and kinetic friction on horizontal surfaces","start":3,"theory":"Static friction adjusts to oppose impending relative sliding, up to a limiting value μsN. It is not automatically at that limit. Once sliding occurs, the simple kinetic model uses μkN. The normal force depends on all perpendicular forces and need not equal mg. Decide whether the object is stationary or sliding before selecting a friction model, and give the friction direction from the actual or impending relative motion.","question":"A 5.00 kg box rests on a level floor with μs = 0.400. A 10.0 N horizontal pull acts. Find friction and test whether it slides using g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["Maximum static friction is 0.400 × 5.00 × 9.80 = 19.6 N","The required balancing friction is only 10.0 N opposite the pull","The box remains at rest because 10.0 N is below the limit"],"claim":"Static friction always equals μsN, even when a smaller force is enough to prevent sliding."},{"title":"Slope components and friction direction","start":10,"theory":"Choose axes parallel and perpendicular to the incline. Weight resolves into mg sinθ downhill and mg cosθ into the plane. Friction opposes relative sliding: a box sliding uphill has downhill friction even if its acceleration is downhill too. Resolving weight simplifies the equations but does not add physical forces. Draw the original weight or its components consistently to avoid double counting.","question":"A box slides uphill on a 20.0° incline with μk = 0.100. Find its acceleration using g = 9.80 m s⁻² and uphill positive.","points":["Both mg sin20.0° and kinetic friction act downhill","a = −g(sin20.0° + 0.100cos20.0°)","Acceleration is −4.27 m s⁻², or 4.27 m s⁻² downhill"],"claim":"Friction always acts uphill on an incline regardless of the direction of sliding."},{"title":"Tension, connected objects and limiting conditions","start":16,"theory":"A massless taut rope over an ideal frictionless pulley transmits a common tension. Analyse the whole connected system to eliminate internal tensions, then isolate a single object to recover the tension. Common acceleration relies on the rope remaining taut and inextensible. At a limiting incline the static-friction inequality becomes an equality, but below the limiting angle the friction force can be smaller.","question":"Two carts of masses 2.00 kg and 3.00 kg are connected on a frictionless track. A 10.0 N force pulls the 3.00 kg cart. Find acceleration and rope tension.","points":["Whole-system acceleration is 10.0/(2.00 + 3.00) = 2.00 m s⁻²","The rope is the only horizontal force on the 2.00 kg cart","Tension is 2.00 × 2.00 = 4.00 N"],"claim":"The rope tension must equal the external 10.0 N pulling force."}],"y11-momentum-impulse":[{"title":"Momentum changes, impulse and rebound signs","start":3,"theory":"Momentum is a vector, so assign a sign before calculating an impulse. Impulse equals final momentum minus initial momentum. A rebound changes direction and can produce a greater momentum change than merely stopping. Using speed magnitudes without directions can therefore severely underestimate the force. The impulse equation describes the net force over the stated time, not necessarily one contact force in isolation.","question":"A 0.200 kg ball changes from +12.0 to −8.00 m s⁻¹. Find the impulse on it.","points":["Initial momentum is +2.40 kg m s⁻¹ and final momentum is −1.60 kg m s⁻¹","Impulse = −1.60 − 2.40 = −4.00 N s","The impulse acts in the negative direction"],"claim":"The impulse during a rebound is found by subtracting the two speed magnitudes."},{"title":"Force–time graphs and average forces","start":11,"theory":"A force pulse need not be constant. Its signed area gives impulse, while the average force is impulse divided by the contact duration. A triangular pulse has half the area of a rectangle with the same peak and duration. Distinguish peak force from average force when interpreting collision data. If gravity is significant, its impulse must be included in the net-force account.","question":"A triangular net-force pulse has peak 600 N and duration 0.0200 s. Find impulse and average force.","points":["Impulse = ½ × 600 × 0.0200 = 6.00 N s","Average force = 6.00/0.0200 = 300 N","The peak force is twice the average for this triangular pulse"],"claim":"Average force equals the maximum force shown on every force–time graph."},{"title":"Collision safety and contact versus net force","start":15,"theory":"For a fixed momentum change, increasing stopping time reduces average net force. Crumple zones, helmets and airbags achieve this by deforming or permitting a longer controlled stopping distance. The momentum change is not eliminated. For a vertical impact, the contact force and weight both contribute to the net force; use the free-body diagram rather than assuming contact force equals Δp/Δt.","question":"A 60.0 kg passenger slows from 10.0 m s⁻¹ to rest. Compare average net force for stopping times 0.100 s and 0.500 s.","points":["The momentum-change magnitude is 60.0 × 10.0 = 600 N s in both cases","Force magnitudes are 6000 N and 1200 N respectively","The longer stop reduces average force by a factor of five without changing the impulse"],"claim":"An airbag protects the passenger by making the required momentum change zero."}],"y11-collisions":[{"title":"System boundaries and sticking collisions","start":3,"theory":"Conservation of momentum requires negligible net external impulse for the selected system during the interaction. The large collision forces are internal when both colliding objects are included. A sticking collision produces a shared final velocity, but does not normally conserve kinetic energy. The algebraic momentum sum must retain direction signs for every object before and after collision.","question":"A 1.00 kg cart at +6.00 m s⁻¹ sticks to a stationary 2.00 kg cart on a frictionless track. Find their shared speed.","points":["Initial total momentum is 1.00 × 6.00 = 6.00 kg m s⁻¹","Final momentum is (1.00 + 2.00)v","The shared velocity is +2.00 m s⁻¹"],"claim":"Momentum cannot be conserved in a collision unless kinetic energy is also conserved."},{"title":"Elasticity and kinetic-energy changes","start":10,"theory":"Momentum conservation alone does not determine whether a collision is elastic. Calculate the total kinetic energy of all included objects before and after the event. An inelastic collision transfers some mechanical energy to deformation, sound and internal energy. These transfers do not violate overall energy conservation. Comparing only one cart's kinetic energy is insufficient because energy can be exchanged between carts.","question":"For the 1.00 kg cart at 6.00 m s⁻¹ sticking to a stationary 2.00 kg cart at final 2.00 m s⁻¹, calculate the kinetic energy lost.","points":["Initial kinetic energy is ½ × 1.00 × 6.00² = 18.0 J","Final kinetic energy is ½ × 3.00 × 2.00² = 6.00 J","The 12.0 J decrease shows an inelastic collision, with energy transferred to other forms"],"claim":"An inelastic collision destroys energy rather than changing its form."},{"title":"Recoil, separation and evaluating experiments","start":15,"theory":"An initially stationary isolated system has zero total momentum. After separation its components have equal and opposite momenta, rather than necessarily equal and opposite velocities. Smaller masses therefore acquire greater speed magnitudes. In experiments, choose a short collision interval to minimise external frictional impulse, measure both carts, and consider uncertainty before judging a small apparent discrepancy.","question":"A stationary 4.00 kg launcher ejects a 0.0200 kg projectile at +100 m s⁻¹ measured in the laboratory. Find launcher recoil velocity.","points":["Initial total momentum is zero","4.00v + 0.0200 × 100 = 0","Launcher velocity is −0.500 m s⁻¹ opposite the projectile"],"claim":"Separated objects must have equal speeds because their momenta are equal and opposite."}],"y11-work-energy-power":[{"title":"Work, kinetic energy and force angles","start":3,"theory":"Work by a constant force uses the component parallel to displacement. A perpendicular force can redirect motion without doing work. Friction does negative work on a sliding object when it opposes displacement. The work–energy theorem uses net work to calculate change in kinetic energy. This is often faster than finding acceleration and time, but the physical system and all relevant work terms still matter.","question":"A 20.0 N force pulls a trolley through 5.00 m at 60.0° to its displacement. Calculate work by this force.","points":["W = Fs cosθ","W = 20.0 × 5.00 × cos60.0° = 50.0 J","Only the force component parallel to displacement contributes"],"claim":"Any force does work equal to its magnitude multiplied by the displacement, regardless of angle."},{"title":"Mechanical-energy budgets and friction","start":11,"theory":"Mechanical energy combines kinetic and potential energy. It is conserved when nonconservative transfers are negligible, while total energy is always conserved for an isolated system. An energy bar chart helps reveal where energy goes. A zero reference for gravitational potential energy is a choice; changes are physically meaningful. Include frictional work or thermal energy explicitly rather than equating initial and final mechanical energies in every situation.","question":"A 2.00 kg object falls 3.00 m from rest and loses 10.0 J to resistance. Find its final kinetic energy with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["Gravitational potential energy decrease is 2.00 × 9.80 × 3.00 = 58.8 J","Final kinetic energy is 58.8 − 10.0 = 48.8 J","The lost mechanical energy becomes other forms; total energy is conserved"],"claim":"Mechanical energy is conserved during every motion even when friction transfers energy to the surroundings."},{"title":"Power, efficiency and interpreting output","start":17,"theory":"Power is an energy-transfer rate, not an amount of energy. Two devices can perform the same work at different powers if the times differ. Efficiency depends on the stated useful output and cannot exceed 100% for an ordinary energy-conversion device. Track consistent units when using kilowatts and kilojoules. A higher input power does not automatically mean a higher efficiency.","question":"A motor lifts a 20.0 kg load through 4.00 m in 5.00 s while using 250 W electrical input. Find useful power and efficiency with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["Useful energy is 20.0 × 9.80 × 4.00 = 784 J","Useful power is 784/5.00 = 156.8 W","Efficiency is 156.8/250 × 100% = 62.7%"],"claim":"Power measures the total energy stored in a device rather than the rate of transfer."}],"y11-wave-foundations":[{"title":"Wave types and the direction of energy transfer","start":3,"theory":"The medium's particles oscillate locally while a wave disturbance travels. Compare the particle oscillation direction with the propagation direction to classify transverse and longitudinal waves. A longitudinal wave's displacement graph can look sinusoidal without making the physical particle motion transverse. Mechanical waves require a medium; electromagnetic waves do not.","question":"Describe particle motion and energy transfer in a sound wave travelling through still air.","points":["Air particles oscillate parallel to the propagation direction","The particles move about equilibrium positions without travelling with the wave over long distances","Energy is transferred through the medium by the propagating disturbance"],"claim":"Air particles travel all the way from the sound source to the listener with the sound wave."},{"title":"Period, frequency, wavelength and speed","start":8,"theory":"Period is time per cycle and frequency is cycles per second. Count complete oscillations and use the elapsed interval between corresponding positions. Wave speed depends on the medium; wavelength adjusts when source frequency changes. In v = fλ, convert centimetres or millimetres to metres before calculating an SI speed.","question":"A wave completes 40 oscillations in 10.0 s and has wavelength 0.750 m. Find frequency, period and speed.","points":["f = 40/10.0 = 4.00 Hz","T = 1/f = 0.250 s","v = fλ = 4.00 × 0.750 = 3.00 m s⁻¹"],"claim":"The period equals the number of oscillations divided by the elapsed time."},{"title":"Displacement graphs and wave measurements","start":13,"theory":"A displacement–distance graph is a snapshot of different positions at one time, while a displacement–time graph records one position over time. Crest separation on the first gives wavelength; repeat time on the second gives period. Neither graph alone normally provides wave speed unless another quantity is known. The vertical scale gives amplitude in both cases.","question":"A distance graph shows adjacent crests 0.80 m apart, and a time graph at one point shows adjacent crests 0.20 s apart. Calculate speed and identify what each separation measures.","points":["The spatial separation is wavelength λ = 0.80 m","The temporal separation is period T = 0.20 s","Wave speed is λ/T = 4.0 m s⁻¹"],"claim":"Crest separation on a displacement–time graph measures wavelength."}],"y11-wave-behaviour":[{"title":"Reflection angles and echo travel time","start":3,"theory":"Measure incidence and reflection angles from the normal, not the surface. Echo timing includes the outward and return paths, so the distance to the reflector is half the wave's total travel distance. A change in reflected pulse orientation depends on the boundary conditions; reflection is not equivalent to stopping the energy transfer.","question":"An echo returns 0.600 s after a sound pulse. With sound speed 340 m s⁻¹, find the wall distance and explain the factor of two.","points":["Total travel distance is vt = 340 × 0.600 = 204 m","The sound travels to the wall and back","Wall distance is 204/2 = 102 m"],"claim":"The wall distance in an echo question is always the full product vt."},{"title":"Refraction and diffraction at boundaries","start":9,"theory":"Source frequency stays fixed when a wave crosses a stationary boundary. If wave speed changes, wavelength changes accordingly. Refraction changes direction at oblique incidence, whereas diffraction is spreading around obstacles or through openings. Diffraction is most noticeable when an opening is comparable to the wavelength. These processes can occur together.","question":"A 500 Hz sound wave enters a medium where speed is 1500 m s⁻¹. Find its wavelength and explain whether frequency changes.","points":["Frequency stays at 500 Hz because the source sets it","Wavelength is v/f = 1500/500 = 3.00 m","Changing medium changes speed and wavelength rather than the source frequency"],"claim":"A wave's frequency must change whenever it enters a different medium."},{"title":"Ultrasound, seismic signals and model evaluation","start":15,"theory":"Pulse-echo techniques infer position from propagation time and an assumed wave speed. Use the speed in the actual material, and distinguish one-way arrival times from round trips. Seismic arrival differences can constrain source distance because different wave types travel at different speeds. A useful answer also identifies uncertainty from timing, changing media or simplified straight paths.","question":"An ultrasound echo returns after 80.0 μs through tissue at 1540 m s⁻¹. Calculate reflector depth.","points":["80.0 μs = 8.00 × 10⁻⁵ s","Depth = vt/2 = 1540 × 8.00 × 10⁻⁵/2","Depth = 0.0616 m, or 6.16 cm"],"claim":"An ultrasound pulse-echo delay is a one-way travel time."}],"y11-wave-interference":[{"title":"Superposition and signed pulse displacement","start":3,"theory":"Add instantaneous displacements algebraically when waves overlap in a linear medium. The resulting displacement can be larger, smaller or zero. Temporary cancellation does not mean the pulses permanently disappear; they continue after overlap in the ideal model. Amplitude describes a maximum, while instantaneous displacement may have either sign.","question":"Two pulses overlap with displacements +3.0 cm and −2.0 cm at one point. Find the resultant and describe what happens after overlap.","points":["Resultant displacement is +3.0 + (−2.0) = +1.0 cm","The overlap is partially destructive","In a linear medium the pulses continue after the overlap without being destroyed"],"claim":"Destructive interference permanently destroys the two interfering waves."},{"title":"Coherence, phase and path difference","start":9,"theory":"A stable interference pattern needs a stable relative phase. For coherent in-phase sources, a whole-number wavelength path difference gives constructive interference; a half-integer gives destructive interference. Path difference is not the total distance from a source. Check the initial phase assumption before using these rules, because out-of-phase sources reverse the conditions.","question":"Two in-phase speakers produce sound of wavelength 0.800 m. Their paths to a listener differ by 1.20 m. Predict the interference.","points":["Path difference divided by wavelength is 1.20/0.800 = 1.50","A half-integer wavelength difference gives destructive interference for in-phase sources","The waves arrive in opposite phase at the listener"],"claim":"A whole-number wavelength path difference gives destructive interference for in-phase sources."},{"title":"Beats and tuning a sound source","start":15,"theory":"Nearby frequencies drift in and out of phase, producing periodic changes in resultant amplitude. Beat frequency is the magnitude of the frequency difference. A measured beat frequency alone leaves an ambiguity: the unknown source may be above or below the reference. Changing the unknown frequency and observing the beat-rate response can resolve this ambiguity.","question":"A 440 Hz reference produces 6 beats per second with a tuning fork. Give both possible fork frequencies and explain why two values are possible.","points":["Beat frequency is the absolute difference between the frequencies","Possible frequencies are 434 Hz and 446 Hz","A difference magnitude does not specify whether the fork is below or above the reference"],"claim":"A beat frequency of 6 Hz means the tuning fork itself oscillates at 6 Hz."}],"y11-standing-waves":[{"title":"Nodes, antinodes and harmonics on strings","start":3,"theory":"Standing waves arise from oppositely travelling waves of equal frequency in the ideal model. Nodes remain at zero displacement and antinodes have maximum oscillation amplitude. Adjacent nodes are separated by half a wavelength. A string fixed at both ends has displacement nodes at both boundaries, constraining the wavelengths that fit.","question":"A 1.20 m string fixed at both ends vibrates in its third harmonic at wave speed 120 m s⁻¹. Find wavelength and frequency.","points":["For the third harmonic L = 3λ/2","λ = 2L/3 = 0.800 m","f = v/λ = 150 Hz"],"claim":"Adjacent displacement nodes in a standing wave are one full wavelength apart."},{"title":"Open and closed air columns","start":10,"theory":"For air displacement, an open end is approximately an antinode and a closed end a node. A pipe closed at one end fits odd numbers of quarter wavelengths, so only odd harmonics appear in the ideal model. Pressure nodes and displacement nodes occur at different locations. State which variable your diagram represents rather than mixing the two.","question":"A pipe closed at one end has length 0.250 m. With sound speed 340 m s⁻¹, find its fundamental and next allowed harmonic frequency.","points":["Fundamental wavelength is 4L = 1.00 m","Fundamental frequency is 340 Hz","The next allowed mode is the third harmonic at 1020 Hz"],"claim":"A pipe closed at one end has every integer harmonic just like an open pipe."},{"title":"Resonance, pitch and boundary conditions","start":17,"theory":"Resonance occurs when driving frequency is near a system's natural frequency, allowing a large response limited by damping. It does not mean all energy is created by the resonating object. Pitch depends on frequency, while loudness also depends on amplitude and perception. Changing length, tension or the medium can change an instrument's allowed frequencies.","question":"A fixed-end string's length is halved while wave speed stays unchanged. Predict the change in fundamental frequency and explain why resonance needs a driving source.","points":["For the fundamental f = v/(2L)","Halving L doubles the fundamental frequency","A driver supplies energy; resonance transfers that energy efficiently rather than creating it"],"claim":"Resonance creates energy within the vibrating object without an external source."}],"y11-nuclear-structure":[{"title":"Nuclides, isotopes and the nuclear model","start":3,"theory":"Atomic number counts protons and determines the element. Mass number counts protons plus neutrons, not the atom's mass in kilograms. Isotopes share proton number but differ in neutron number. Electron loss changes the ion's charge without changing the nuclear identity. Scattering evidence supports a small, dense nucleus rather than a uniformly distributed positive charge.","question":"A neutral atom has atomic number 13 and mass number 27. State its proton, neutron and electron numbers, then describe its 3+ ion.","points":["The neutral atom has 13 protons and 27 − 13 = 14 neutrons","It has 13 electrons when neutral","Its 3+ ion has 10 electrons while the nucleus is unchanged"],"claim":"Removing electrons changes an atom into an isotope of a different element."},{"title":"Nuclear stability, quarks and spontaneous decay","start":9,"theory":"The strong interaction binds nearby nucleons against electrostatic proton repulsion. Its short range helps explain why very large nuclei can be unstable. Radioactive decay is spontaneous and stochastic: a half-life predicts population behaviour, not the exact decay time of a named nucleus. Protons and neutrons contain different combinations of up and down quarks.","question":"Using charges +⅔e for an up quark and −⅓e for a down quark, show the charge of a proton uud and neutron udd.","points":["Proton charge is ⅔e + ⅔e − ⅓e = +e","Neutron charge is ⅔e − ⅓e − ⅓e = 0","Different quark combinations explain their different net charges"],"claim":"A neutron contains no electrically charged constituents because its total charge is zero."},{"title":"Alpha, beta and gamma decay mechanisms","start":12,"theory":"Alpha emission removes two protons and two neutrons. Beta-minus converts a neutron into a proton, while beta-plus converts a proton into a neutron; the mass number stays constant. Neutrinos participate in beta processes and help account for energy and momentum. Gamma emission changes nuclear energy state without changing proton or neutron numbers.","question":"An unstable nucleus undergoes beta-minus decay. State the changes in mass number and atomic number and identify the nucleon conversion.","points":["Mass number is unchanged","Atomic number increases by one","A neutron becomes a proton with emission of an electron and an electron antineutrino"],"claim":"Beta-minus emission reduces the nucleus's atomic number because an electron is emitted."}],"y11-nuclear-equations-half-life":[{"title":"Balancing nuclear equations","start":3,"theory":"Conserve total mass number and electric charge in a nuclear equation. A superscript zero for beta radiation does not mean an electron has zero mass; it means it is not a nucleon. Identify the emitted particle and check the daughter element against its atomic number. Successive decays require updating the nucleus after each stage.","question":"A nucleus with A = 210 and Z = 84 emits an alpha particle, then undergoes beta-minus decay. Give the final A and Z.","points":["Alpha emission gives A = 206 and Z = 82","Beta-minus leaves A unchanged and raises Z by one","Final values are A = 206 and Z = 83"],"claim":"Every nuclear decay decreases the mass number."},{"title":"Half-life, population fractions and decay curves","start":8,"theory":"Each half-life halves the remaining undecayed population, rather than removing a fixed number. Activity follows the same exponential decrease for a single isolated nuclide. Identify elapsed time and divide by half-life to count intervals. A smooth decay curve describes an expected trend; actual counts fluctuate statistically.","question":"A source's corrected activity is initially 960 Bq and its half-life is 3.00 h. Find activity after 9.00 h.","points":["The elapsed time contains 9.00/3.00 = 3 half-lives","Remaining fraction is (½)³ = ⅛","Activity is 960/8 = 120 Bq"],"claim":"A radioactive source loses the same number of undecayed nuclei in every half-life."},{"title":"Activity, background correction and experimental inference","start":15,"theory":"Detector count rate need not equal source activity because detection efficiency and geometry matter. Background counts add to the source measurement and should be subtracted before inferring a half-life. Keep measurement durations comparable, collect sufficient counts and use a best-fit decay trend. Treat random count fluctuation differently from a steady background offset.","question":"A detector records 220 counts per minute initially and 120 counts per minute after 5.0 min. Background is 20 counts per minute. Determine the half-life.","points":["Corrected source rates are 200 and 100 counts per minute","The source rate has halved","Half-life is 5.0 min under unchanged detector geometry"],"claim":"Half-life can be found by halving the uncorrected count rate even when background is significant."}],"y11-radiation-dose":[{"title":"Ionisation, shielding and field deflection","start":3,"theory":"Ionising radiation can remove bound electrons and alter molecules. Alpha radiation is strongly ionising over a short range; gamma is less directly ionising and highly penetrating. Charged alpha and beta radiation deflect in electric fields, while gamma does not. Shielding effectiveness depends on radiation type, energy and thickness, so a universal thin barrier is not adequate.","question":"An electric field points right. Predict the force direction on alpha, beta-minus and gamma radiation and identify which has no electric deflection.","points":["Alpha is positively charged so its electric force is right","Beta-minus is negatively charged so its electric force is left","Gamma is uncharged and has no electric deflection"],"claim":"Gamma radiation has negative charge and bends in the same direction as beta-minus radiation."},{"title":"Exposure, contamination and radiation detection","start":9,"theory":"Irradiation is exposure to radiation; contamination places radioactive material on or inside an object. Removing the external source ends irradiation but may not remove contamination. Internal alpha emitters can be hazardous despite alpha's low external penetration. Detector readings require background correction and a consistent setup to support a fair comparison.","question":"Explain why an alpha emitter outside intact skin and the same emitter inhaled can present different risks.","points":["External alpha particles have limited penetration and can be stopped by outer skin","Inhalation places the source close to living internal tissue","Strong ionisation deposits energy over a short range inside the body"],"claim":"Alpha radiation cannot be hazardous because paper can stop it."},{"title":"Absorbed dose, radiation weighting and applications","start":13,"theory":"Absorbed dose is energy deposited per kilogram of tissue, measured in gray. A radiation weighting factor accounts for different biological effects in the course equivalent-dose model, giving sievert units. Dose is not source activity: exposure duration, geometry, shielding and absorption also matter. Application questions should connect radiation properties with both usefulness and protection measures.","question":"A tissue sample of mass 0.0200 kg absorbs 0.00400 J. Find absorbed dose and equivalent dose with weighting factor 20.","points":["Absorbed dose D = E/m = 0.200 Gy","Equivalent dose H = wD = 20 × 0.200","H = 4.00 Sv in this stated weighting model"],"claim":"A source's activity in becquerels is itself the absorbed dose in gray."}],"y11-nuclear-energy":[{"title":"Mass defect and binding energy calculations","start":3,"theory":"A bound nucleus has lower rest mass than its separated constituent nucleons. The positive mass defect corresponds to energy released when the bound state forms, and energy required to separate it. Use consistent atomic or nuclear masses so electron masses are treated consistently. Binding energy per nucleon makes comparisons between different-sized nuclei meaningful.","question":"A nucleus has mass defect 0.0300 u and contains four nucleons. Use 1 u c² = 931.5 MeV to find total and per-nucleon binding energy.","points":["Total binding energy is 0.0300 × 931.5 = 27.945 MeV","Per-nucleon binding energy is 27.945/4 = 6.99 MeV","Binding energy is needed to separate the bound nucleus into free nucleons"],"claim":"A bound nucleus has more rest mass than the sum of its separated nucleons."},{"title":"Fission, chain reactions and reactor systems","start":11,"theory":"Fission releases energy when products are more tightly bound per nucleon than the initial heavy nucleus. Emitted neutrons can trigger further fissions, but a controlled reactor needs the neutron population regulated. A moderator slows neutrons; control rods absorb them. Coolant removes energy, while shielding reduces radiation exposure. These functions are related but not interchangeable.","question":"Explain the distinct roles of moderator, control rods and coolant in a thermal-neutron fission reactor.","points":["The moderator slows neutrons to improve the intended fission interaction probability","Control rods absorb neutrons to regulate the chain reaction","Coolant carries thermal energy away from the reactor core"],"claim":"A moderator controls a reactor by absorbing all emitted neutrons."},{"title":"Fusion, stellar energy and comparing nuclear systems","start":18,"theory":"Fusion of light nuclei can release energy because the products have a greater binding energy per nucleon. The positive nuclei repel electrically, so high temperatures help overcome their approach barrier. Energy release still follows the reactant-minus-product mass difference. Comparing fusion and fission requires considering operating conditions, waste, fuel and engineering rather than assuming all nuclear processes are identical.","question":"A fusion reaction has reactant mass greater than product mass by 0.0200 u. Use 1 u c² = 931.5 MeV to find released energy and explain its origin.","points":["Released energy is 0.0200 × 931.5 = 18.6 MeV","The final bound products have lower rest mass","The mass difference corresponds to increased kinetic and other released energy"],"claim":"Fusion releases energy because nuclear mass is not conserved in any form and energy is created from nothing."}],"y11-charge-current-voltage":[{"title":"Charge, carriers and material behaviour","start":3,"theory":"Net charge reflects an imbalance between positive and negative charges. Charging transfers or redistributes charge rather than creating it. In metals, mobile electrons provide charge transport while positive ions remain in the lattice. A neutral object can be attracted through polarisation, so attraction alone does not prove opposite net charge. Material behaviour depends on the availability and mobility of carriers.","question":"A neutral metal object gains 5.00 × 10¹² electrons. Using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, find its net charge and describe the transfer.","points":["Charge magnitude is Ne = 8.00 × 10⁻⁷ C","The object's charge is −8.00 × 10⁻⁷ C because it gained electrons","Charge was transferred from another body, not created"],"claim":"A neutral conductor contains no electric charges."},{"title":"Current, electron count and conventional direction","start":8,"theory":"Current is the rate at which charge crosses a section of conductor. Electron drift in a metal is opposite to conventional current. Charge conservation prevents a steady series current being used up by components; energy is transferred instead. For varying current, charge is the area under the current–time graph. Always distinguish charge in coulombs from a count of electrons.","question":"A steady current of 0.400 A flows for 25.0 s. Find charge and electron count using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.","points":["Q = It = 0.400 × 25.0 = 10.0 C","Number of electrons is Q/e","N = 6.25 × 10¹⁹ electrons"],"claim":"Current is consumed as charges pass through a lamp."},{"title":"Potential difference and correct meter placement","start":15,"theory":"Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge between two points. It is not the force on a charge or the total energy by itself. A voltmeter compares two points in parallel; an ammeter measures branch current in series. Ideal voltmeters have very large resistance and ideal ammeters very small resistance so their effects on the circuit are limited.","question":"A component transfers 36.0 J as 3.00 C passes through it. Find potential difference and explain the correct voltmeter connection.","points":["V = E/Q = 36.0/3.00 = 12.0 V","Connect the voltmeter across the component's two terminals","Parallel connection measures the potential difference between the terminals"],"claim":"An ideal voltmeter should be connected in series to measure component voltage."}],"y11-resistance-energy-power":[{"title":"Resistance, Ohm's law and graph conventions","start":3,"theory":"Resistance is V/I at the operating point. An ohmic conductor maintains a constant ratio at constant physical conditions, giving a straight V–I relationship through the origin. A curved graph indicates changing resistance or non-ohmic behaviour. The gradient equals resistance only when voltage is vertical and current horizontal; reversed axes give conductance. Heating can change the component's behaviour during measurement.","question":"A V–I graph has voltage on the vertical axis and passes through (0.200 A, 3.00 V) and (0.600 A, 9.00 V). Find its gradient and interpretation.","points":["Gradient = (9.00 − 3.00)/(0.600 − 0.200) = 15.0 V A⁻¹","V A⁻¹ is equivalent to ohms","A straight line through the origin indicates constant resistance under the measured conditions"],"claim":"The gradient is resistance regardless of which variable is placed on each graph axis."},{"title":"Electrical energy, power and controlled comparisons","start":14,"theory":"Power can be written VI, I²R or V²/R using appropriate relations. The comparison depends on what is held constant: increasing resistance reduces power at fixed voltage but increases it at fixed current. Energy equals power multiplied by duration. Do not use a component's rated power at an arbitrary different voltage without considering its operating model.","question":"A 12.0 V supply powers a 6.00 Ω resistor. Calculate current, power and energy in 20.0 s.","points":["I = V/R = 2.00 A","P = VI = 24.0 W","E = Pt = 480 J"],"claim":"Increasing resistance always increases power without regard to the supply conditions."},{"title":"Kilowatt-hours, efficiency and practical evaluation","start":19,"theory":"A kilowatt-hour is an energy unit because it multiplies power by time. Convert watts to kilowatts before calculating energy in kWh, or convert both factors to SI units for joules. Cost depends on energy use and tariff rather than power alone. An Ohm's law investigation needs control of temperature, correct meter connections and uncertainty estimates.","question":"A 1500 W heater runs for 40.0 min. Find energy use in kWh and cost at $0.30 per kWh.","points":["1500 W = 1.50 kW and 40.0 min = ⅔ h","Energy use is 1.00 kWh","Cost is $0.30"],"claim":"A kilowatt-hour is a unit of power rather than energy."}],"y11-series-parallel-circuits":[{"title":"Junctions, loops and series networks","start":3,"theory":"Circuit connections are determined by nodes, not how neatly the wires are drawn. At a steady-state junction, current entering equals current leaving because charge does not continually accumulate. Around a loop, potential rises and drops balance. Components in series share current, while their voltage drops depend on resistance. Label nodes before deciding how to simplify a network.","question":"A 12.0 V ideal supply is connected to 2.00 Ω and 4.00 Ω resistors in series. Find current and the two voltage drops.","points":["Total resistance is 6.00 Ω, so I = 2.00 A","The 2.00 Ω resistor drops 4.00 V","The 4.00 Ω resistor drops 8.00 V, and the drops sum to 12.0 V"],"claim":"Each series resistor receives the full supply voltage."},{"title":"Parallel branches and compound reduction","start":11,"theory":"Parallel branches connect to the same two nodes and therefore share potential difference. Branch currents may differ according to branch resistance, while the total current is their sum. The equivalent resistance is smaller than the smallest branch resistance. For compound networks reduce identifiable sections, find total current, then expand the network to recover branch values.","question":"A 6.00 Ω resistor and a 3.00 Ω resistor are connected in parallel across 12.0 V. Find branch currents and equivalent resistance.","points":["Branch currents are 12.0/6.00 = 2.00 A and 12.0/3.00 = 4.00 A","Total current is 6.00 A","Equivalent resistance is 12.0/6.00 = 2.00 Ω"],"claim":"All branches of a parallel circuit carry the same current even when their resistances differ."},{"title":"Voltage dividers and fault reasoning","start":19,"theory":"A divider shares voltage across series resistors in proportion to resistance. Specify the output terminals because swapping the measured resistor reverses a sensor's response. The simple divider equation assumes negligible output loading. An open parallel branch removes one conducting path, whereas a short can greatly reduce equivalent resistance. Analyse nodes and paths instead of relying on visual proximity.","question":"A 9.00 V supply feeds a 2.00 kΩ upper resistor and a 1.00 kΩ lower resistor in series. Find voltage across the lower resistor and predict the effect of increasing its resistance.","points":["Vout = 9.00 × 1.00/(2.00 + 1.00) = 3.00 V","The output fraction is Rlower/(Rupper + Rlower)","Increasing the lower resistance increases Vout for this unloaded divider"],"claim":"A divider's output always decreases when the lower resistor's resistance increases."}],"y11-components-ac-safety":[{"title":"Diodes, LEDs and sensor-divider circuits","start":3,"theory":"A diode conducts preferentially in one direction. An LED also needs current limitation; the supply voltage is shared between the LED and its series resistor. Sensors convert environmental changes into electrical changes. In a sensor divider, determine the output terminals and resistance response before predicting whether voltage rises or falls. A resistance change does not by itself specify an output direction.","question":"An LED has 2.00 V forward drop at 0.0200 A on a 6.00 V supply. Find the required series resistance.","points":["Resistor voltage is 6.00 − 2.00 = 4.00 V","R = V/I = 4.00/0.0200","Required resistance is 200 Ω in this stated operating model"],"claim":"The LED's series resistor receives the full supply voltage even while the LED is conducting."},{"title":"AC, RMS values and transmission losses","start":12,"theory":"AC reverses direction periodically. Frequency counts cycles per second, while RMS gives the DC-equivalent heating effect for a resistive load. For a sinusoid, RMS voltage is peak voltage divided by √2. High-voltage transmission reduces current for a given transferred power, reducing I²R losses. It does not make line resistance vanish.","question":"An ideal line transfers 20.0 kW at 10.0 kV through total line resistance 5.00 Ω. Calculate current and resistive loss.","points":["I = P/V = 20000/10000 = 2.00 A","Line power loss is I²R = 2.00² × 5.00 = 20.0 W","Higher transmission voltage lowers current and therefore resistive losses for fixed transferred power"],"claim":"High-voltage transmission works by reducing the physical resistance of the cables to zero."},{"title":"Earthing, overcurrent devices and RCD protection","start":17,"theory":"A protective earth provides a low-resistance fault path from exposed metalwork. Fuses and circuit breakers respond to overcurrent. An RCD compares outgoing and returning currents and trips on sufficient imbalance, which can indicate leakage. These devices have distinct functions and do not guarantee safety in every fault. Double insulation uses two protective barriers without relying on an earth connection.","question":"Compare the functions of a fuse, a protective earth and an RCD in a faulty metal-cased appliance.","points":["A fuse interrupts the circuit when excessive current melts its element","Protective earth provides a low-resistance path for a live-to-case fault","An RCD detects sufficient imbalance between active and neutral currents and disconnects the supply"],"claim":"An RCD is simply a fuse that trips whenever the total current is large."}],"static-equilibrium":[{"title":"Centre of mass, stability and torque","start":1,"theory":"A body's weight acts through its centre of mass in a uniform gravitational field. Stability depends on whether the line of action remains within the support base. Torque uses perpendicular distance to the force's line of action, not simply the lever's length. Tilting can move the line of action beyond an edge, allowing weight to create a toppling torque.","question":"A 30.0 N force acts perpendicular to a lever 0.400 m from a pivot. Find torque and predict the torque if its line of action passes through the pivot.","points":["Torque magnitude is 30.0 × 0.400 = 12.0 N m","A force through the pivot has zero perpendicular lever arm","Its torque about that pivot is zero even though the force is nonzero"],"claim":"Every nonzero force creates a nonzero torque about every pivot."},{"title":"Force balance and supported beams","start":8,"theory":"A rigid body needs both zero net force and zero net torque for static equilibrium. Taking moments about a support can remove an unknown reaction from the moment equation without implying that its force is zero. Distributed beam weight acts at the beam's centre of mass. Calculate another support reaction from vertical force balance after solving the torque equation.","question":"A uniform 4.00 m beam weighs 200 N and is supported at both ends. A 100 N load is 1.00 m from the left end. Find both upward reactions.","points":["About the left support: 4.00RB = 200 × 2.00 + 100 × 1.00","Right reaction RB = 125 N","Vertical balance gives left reaction RA = 300 − 125 = 175 N"],"claim":"Choosing a support as the moment pivot means its reaction force is absent from the force balance."},{"title":"Cables, ladders and equilibrium assumptions","start":11,"theory":"Draw the complete free-body diagram before taking moments. A frictionless wall supplies only a normal reaction perpendicular to the wall. A supporting cable pulls along its length and never pushes. Resolve angled forces for force balance, or use perpendicular lever arms for moments. Check whether an assumed contact reaction remains positive; a negative result can indicate loss of contact or an invalid physical arrangement.","question":"A 200 N sign hangs at rest from two symmetric cables, each 30.0° above horizontal. Find tension in each and explain the horizontal balance.","points":["Vertical balance gives 2T sin30.0° = 200 N","Each cable tension is 200 N","Equal horizontal components act oppositely and cancel"],"claim":"Each supporting cable's tension equals half the sign's weight regardless of cable angle."}],"inclined-planes":[{"title":"Weight components and frictionless slope motion","start":1,"theory":"Gravity acts vertically even when the chosen axes follow a slope. Resolve it once into perpendicular components, keeping the incline angle's geometry clear. With no other perpendicular force, N = mg cosθ. The net downhill force on a frictionless slope is mg sinθ, so mass cancels from acceleration. The normal force remains physically real while doing no work along the ideal surface.","question":"A 2.00 kg cart descends a frictionless 30.0° slope. Find normal force and acceleration with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["N = mg cos30.0° = 17.0 N","Downhill net force is mg sin30.0° = 9.80 N","Acceleration is 4.90 m s⁻² downhill"],"claim":"The normal force on every incline is equal to mg."},{"title":"Frictional slopes and driven motion","start":8,"theory":"Friction direction follows relative sliding, not necessarily acceleration. A vehicle climbing steadily has zero acceleration but still requires a driving force to balance gravity and resistance. For sliding down, subtract kinetic friction from the downhill weight component. Compare the necessary static friction with its maximum before deciding that an object will move.","question":"A box slides down a 30.0° slope with μk = 0.200. Find acceleration with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["The downhill weight component per unit mass is g sin30.0°","Kinetic friction per unit mass is μkg cos30.0° uphill","a = 9.80(0.500 − 0.200 × 0.866) = 3.20 m s⁻² downhill"],"claim":"Friction always opposes the acceleration rather than relative sliding."},{"title":"Angled pulls, energy checks and multi-stage slopes","start":15,"theory":"An angled pull can reduce or increase the normal reaction, changing the friction force. Resolve the applied force in both axes. Check a dynamics result using work and energy over a known distance: the normal does no work along the surface, while friction reduces mechanical energy. Multi-stage motion needs a new force or energy account whenever the surface or applied force changes.","question":"A 4.00 kg box is pulled horizontally by 20.0 N at 30.0° above horizontal on a floor with μk = 0.100. Find acceleration with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["N = mg − F sin30.0° = 29.2 N","Fnet,x = 20.0 cos30.0° − 0.100 × 29.2 = 14.4 N","Acceleration is 14.4/4.00 = 3.60 m s⁻²"],"claim":"An upward component of an applied pull has no effect on kinetic friction."}],"grav-fields":[{"title":"Universal gravitation and inverse-square reasoning","start":1,"theory":"Universal gravitation uses centre-to-centre separation for point masses or appropriate spherical bodies. Each mass experiences the same force magnitude despite possibly different acceleration. Changing separation has a squared effect; changing one mass has a direct effect. Ratio methods often avoid unnecessary arithmetic and expose the underlying relationship more clearly than calculating two large numbers.","question":"Two spherical masses attract with force F. One mass doubles and their centre separation triples. Find the new force in terms of F.","points":["Force is proportional to the product of the masses","Force is inversely proportional to separation squared","New force is 2F/9"],"claim":"Tripling separation reduces gravitational force to one third of its original value."},{"title":"Field strength, altitude and field diagrams","start":6,"theory":"Field strength describes force per unit test mass at a location and is independent of the test mass. Altitude is measured from the surface, whereas r in GM/r² is measured from the centre. Field arrows point toward the source mass. Greater field-line density represents stronger field qualitatively; the lines are a representation, not physical strings.","question":"At altitude equal to a planet's radius R, compare field strength with its surface value g₀.","points":["Centre distance is r = R + R = 2R","Field strength is proportional to 1/r²","The field is g₀/4 directed toward the centre"],"claim":"An altitude of R means r = R in the inverse-square field equation."},{"title":"Superposition and planetary comparisons","start":14,"theory":"Gravitational fields add as vectors. Between two masses their fields point in opposite directions, so a cancellation point can exist; outside the pair their fields may reinforce. For unequal masses the cancellation point lies closer to the smaller mass. For different planets, surface field depends on both mass and radius, not density or size alone.","question":"Planet B has twice Earth's mass and twice Earth's radius. Find its surface field in terms of Earth's surface field.","points":["g = GM/R²","The mass factor is 2 and the radius-squared factor is 4","Planet B has surface field gEarth/2"],"claim":"A planet twice as massive must have twice the surface field regardless of its radius."}],"grav-energy":[{"title":"Near-Earth work and the choice of potential reference","start":1,"theory":"Near Earth's surface over a modest height interval, treat g as constant and use ΔEp = mgΔh. The reference height is arbitrary; physical predictions depend on changes. Work by gravity has the opposite sign to the change in gravitational potential energy. Quasistatic lifting requires positive external work equal to the energy increase when losses and kinetic-energy change are negligible.","question":"A 3.00 kg object is slowly raised 2.00 m. Find its potential-energy change and work by gravity using g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["ΔEp = 3.00 × 9.80 × 2.00 = +58.8 J","Work by gravity is −ΔEp = −58.8 J","Gravity opposes the upward displacement"],"claim":"Work by gravity is positive whenever gravitational potential energy increases."},{"title":"Changing-radius gravitational energy: extension","start":4,"theory":"For large radial changes, gravitational force varies significantly and a constant-g approximation fails. The extension model Ep = −GMm/r sets zero energy at infinity. Negative potential energy represents a bound configuration under this convention. Moving outward makes potential energy less negative. Do not confuse the absolute sign of Ep with the sign of its change.","question":"Using Ep = −GMm/r, an object moves from radius r to 2r. Give the potential-energy change in terms of GMm/r.","points":["Initial potential energy is −GMm/r","Final potential energy is −GMm/(2r)","ΔEp = +GMm/(2r), so external energy is needed for slow outward motion"],"claim":"A negative gravitational potential energy means that moving outward must reduce potential energy further."},{"title":"Energy conservation, escape and model selection: extension","start":12,"theory":"Use kinetic plus gravitational potential energy to compare positions when nonconservative work is negligible. In the infinity-zero convention, zero total energy is the escape threshold with zero final speed at infinity. A negative total energy corresponds to a bound orbit in this model. Label advanced radial-potential calculations as extension and distinguish them from the core near-surface energy relationships.","question":"An object has kinetic energy +8.0 MJ and gravitational potential energy −12.0 MJ in an isolated gravitational system. Is it energetically able to escape without added energy?","points":["Total mechanical energy is −4.0 MJ","Escape with nonnegative final kinetic energy at infinity requires total energy at least zero","At least 4.0 MJ additional energy is needed in the ideal model"],"claim":"Any moving object can escape a planet because its kinetic energy is positive."}],"projectile":[{"title":"Launch components and independent motion","start":1,"theory":"Resolve the launch velocity using the given angle reference. Horizontal velocity stays constant in the ideal no-drag model, while vertical velocity changes under gravity. Independence means the vertical equation does not require the horizontal component, not that the time intervals differ. Both components describe the same object over the same elapsed time.","question":"A projectile launches at 20.0 m s⁻¹, 30.0° above horizontal. Find both initial components and its vertical velocity after 1.00 s with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["ux = 20.0 cos30.0° = 17.3 m s⁻¹","uy = 20.0 sin30.0° = 10.0 m s⁻¹","vy = 10.0 − 9.80 × 1.00 = +0.200 m s⁻¹"],"claim":"Horizontal and vertical projectile motions use different elapsed times for the same flight event."},{"title":"Range and launches from a height","start":9,"theory":"For equal launch and landing heights, flight time is twice the time to maximum height under the ideal model. That symmetry fails for an elevated launch. Use the known vertical displacement to solve the vertical equation first, rejecting nonphysical time roots. Substitute the accepted flight time into horizontal motion rather than using a same-height range formula automatically.","question":"A ball launches horizontally at 8.00 m s⁻¹ from height 4.90 m. Find its flight time and horizontal range with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["4.90 = ½gt² gives t = 1.00 s","Horizontal velocity remains 8.00 m s⁻¹","Range is 8.00 × 1.00 = 8.00 m"],"claim":"A horizontal launch from a cliff can use the equal-height range formula unchanged."},{"title":"Impact vectors, unequal heights and model limits","start":13,"theory":"Impact speed is the magnitude of the final velocity vector, not merely its vertical component. Resolve direction using a stated reference axis. Air resistance can change both components, destroy ideal symmetry and reduce range. For a platform launch, displacement is measured from the launch position; a ground level below it has negative vertical displacement if upward is positive.","question":"A projectile reaches the ground with vx = +12.0 m s⁻¹ and vy = −9.00 m s⁻¹. Calculate impact speed and direction.","points":["Speed is √(12.0² + 9.00²) = 15.0 m s⁻¹","Angle is tan⁻¹(9.00/12.0) = 36.9°","Velocity is 15.0 m s⁻¹ at 36.9° below horizontal in the forward direction"],"claim":"Impact speed equals the vertical velocity component regardless of horizontal motion."}],"circular":[{"title":"Centripetal acceleration and horizontal circles","start":1,"theory":"Constant speed does not imply constant velocity when direction changes. Circular motion needs an inward resultant; centripetal force names this resultant rather than a new interaction. Identify real forces such as tension, friction or gravity in the free-body diagram. The velocity is tangential while radial acceleration points inward, perpendicular to it in uniform circular motion.","question":"A 0.500 kg object moves in a horizontal circle of radius 2.00 m at 4.00 m s⁻¹. Find acceleration and resultant force.","points":["ac = v²/r = 8.00 m s⁻² inward","Fnet = mac = 4.00 N inward","The resultant comes from actual interactions, not an added centripetal-force arrow"],"claim":"Centripetal force is an additional physical force that must be added to tension or friction."},{"title":"Banked turns and conical pendulums","start":9,"theory":"On an ideal frictionless bank, the horizontal component of the normal force supplies the inward resultant while its vertical component balances weight. Dividing those equations gives tanθ = v²/(rg). In a conical pendulum, tension plays the corresponding role. The circular radius is the horizontal distance to the axis, not automatically the rope length.","question":"Find the ideal banking angle for a frictionless 50.0 m radius turn at 15.0 m s⁻¹ with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["tanθ = v²/(rg)","tanθ = 225/(50.0 × 9.80) = 0.459","θ = 24.7°"],"claim":"The bank angle is set by the vehicle's mass in an ideal frictionless turn."},{"title":"Vertical circles, energy and apparent weight","start":15,"theory":"At the top of a vertical circle inward is downward; at the bottom inward is upward. Rebuild the radial force equation at each position rather than memorising a sign. Gravity changes speed around a nonuniform loop, so combine energy with radial dynamics. Contact or string tension cannot be negative in the usual model; zero can signal the limiting contact condition.","question":"A 1.00 kg mass moves at 6.00 m s⁻¹ at the bottom of a vertical circle of radius 2.00 m. Find string tension with g = 9.80 m s⁻².","points":["At the bottom, T − mg = mv²/r","The inward resultant is 1.00 × 36.0/2.00 = 18.0 N","T = 18.0 + 9.80 = 27.8 N"],"claim":"At the bottom of a vertical circle, tension equals mv²/r without accounting for weight."}],"orbits":[{"title":"Kepler's laws and deriving the period relation","start":1,"theory":"For a circular satellite orbit, gravity supplies the inward resultant. Equating GMm/r² to mv²/r cancels satellite mass. Combine the result with v = 2πr/T to derive T²/r³ = 4π²/(GM). Kepler's equal-area law describes changing speed in an elliptical orbit; it does not say that orbital speed is constant everywhere.","question":"Two circular satellites orbit the same planet at radii r and 4r. Compare their periods.","points":["For the same central mass, T² is proportional to r³","The period ratio is 4^(3/2)","The outer satellite's period is eight times the inner satellite's period"],"claim":"Doubling a satellite's own mass doubles its circular orbital period at a fixed radius."},{"title":"Weightlessness and satellite classifications","start":11,"theory":"An orbiting astronaut and spacecraft are both falling under gravity, so a support scale can read approximately zero while gravitational force remains significant. Geosynchronous means matching Earth's rotation period; geostationary additionally requires a circular equatorial orbit in the rotation direction. Polar and sun-synchronous orbits serve different observation purposes. Classify both altitude and inclination when discussing applications.","question":"Explain why a satellite with a 24-hour period is not necessarily geostationary.","points":["Matching the rotation period makes it geosynchronous in the ideal description","A geostationary orbit must also be circular and equatorial","It must travel in Earth's rotation direction to remain above one location"],"claim":"Every satellite with Earth's rotation period remains fixed above one point on the ground."},{"title":"Orbital energy and higher-orbit comparisons","start":17,"theory":"A higher circular orbit has lower orbital speed but greater total mechanical energy in the ideal gravitational model. Energy must be added to transfer between circular orbits even though final speed is smaller. During a real transfer the intermediate path is not simply the new circular orbit. Keep orbital radius distinct from altitude and distinguish transfer dynamics from comparisons of final states.","question":"Compare speed and period for two circular orbits of radii r and 4r around the same planet.","points":["v is proportional to r⁻½, so outer speed is half the inner speed","T is proportional to r^(3/2), so outer period is eight times as long","A larger orbit can have a lower speed and a longer path without contradiction"],"claim":"Moving to a higher circular orbit always requires removing energy because final orbital speed is lower."}],"relativity-foundations":[{"title":"Inertial frames and classical relative motion","start":1,"theory":"An inertial frame is one in which an unforced object moves with constant velocity. Relative velocities need a stated observer and sign convention. Classical velocity addition is an excellent low-speed approximation, but does not apply unchanged when speeds are substantial fractions of c. The same event can have different coordinate descriptions in different frames without either observation being invalid.","question":"A train moves at +20.0 m s⁻¹ relative to the ground. A person walks at −2.00 m s⁻¹ relative to the train. Find ground velocity using the low-speed approximation.","points":["Ground velocity is the sum of the signed relative velocities","v = 20.0 − 2.00 = +18.0 m s⁻¹","The positive sign indicates motion in the train's forward direction relative to the ground"],"claim":"Relative motion has a meaningful velocity without specifying an observer or reference frame."},{"title":"Einstein's postulates and simultaneity","start":5,"theory":"The laws of physics have the same form in all inertial frames, and vacuum light speed is the same for all inertial observers. These are claims about measurements, not merely visual appearance. Synchronisation of separated clocks is frame-dependent. Simultaneous events in one frame can therefore occur at different coordinate times in another. Receiving two light signals together is not alone proof of simultaneous emission.","question":"Explain why two flashes received together by an observer need not have been emitted simultaneously.","points":["Reception times include both emission times and light travel times","Different source distances can produce different travel times","Simultaneity needs a frame-based timing procedure that accounts for propagation"],"claim":"Seeing two flashes at the same instant always proves their emission events were simultaneous."},{"title":"Lorentz factor and testing the classical limit","start":12,"theory":"The Lorentz factor is dimensionless and depends on v/c, not the numerical speed in arbitrary units. It approaches one at low speed and increases sharply near c. This explains why everyday mechanics is a useful limit of relativistic descriptions. Experimental evidence, including high-speed muons, tests the theory rather than allowing the postulates to be treated as unsupported assumptions.","question":"Calculate γ at v = 0.800c and explain its low-speed limit.","points":["γ = 1/√(1 − 0.800²)","γ = 1/0.600 = 1.667","At v much smaller than c, γ approaches one"],"claim":"The Lorentz factor can be less than one for a massive moving object."}],"relativity-effects":[{"title":"Proper time and time-dilation reasoning","start":1,"theory":"Proper time is measured by a clock present at both events at one location in its own frame. Identify the pair of events before choosing the proper interval. Time dilation relates coordinate intervals between those events across frames; it is not simply a delayed view through a telescope. Use consistent frame labels to avoid substituting the longer interval as the proper one.","question":"A particle's proper lifetime is 2.00 μs and γ = 2.50 relative to the laboratory. Find its laboratory lifetime and identify the proper clock.","points":["The particle-frame interval is the proper lifetime","Laboratory lifetime is γΔt₀ = 2.50 × 2.00 = 5.00 μs","The proper clock is at rest with the particle at both relevant events"],"claim":"The longer observed lifetime is always the proper lifetime."},{"title":"Proper length and directional contraction","start":6,"theory":"Proper length is measured in the object's rest frame. A moving-frame length requires simultaneous endpoint positions in that frame and is shorter along the relative-motion direction. Transverse dimensions are unchanged in the simple special-relativity comparison. Contraction is a relationship between frame measurements, not mechanical compression of a rod in its own rest frame.","question":"A spacecraft has proper length 120 m and moves with γ = 1.50. Find its measured length along the motion direction and comment on width.","points":["Moving-frame length is L₀/γ = 120/1.50 = 80.0 m","The spacecraft's rest-frame length remains 120 m","Its transverse width is unchanged by this directional contraction model"],"claim":"Length contraction reduces every dimension of an object by the same factor."},{"title":"Muon evidence and reconciling frames","start":10,"theory":"Earth-frame observers explain penetrating muons with an increased coordinate lifetime. Muon-frame observers explain the same arrival events with a shortened atmospheric distance. These accounts must predict consistent encounters, rather than being rival physical outcomes. Twin scenarios involve changing frames, so a purely symmetric inertial comparison cannot be applied to the complete journey without analysing its events.","question":"A muon moves at 0.800c with proper lifetime 2.20 μs. Using c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and γ = 1.667, find its characteristic Earth-frame travel distance.","points":["Earth-frame lifetime is 1.667 × 2.20 μs = 3.67 μs","Speed is 2.40 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","Distance is approximately 880 m; this is a characteristic distance, not a guaranteed distance for each muon"],"claim":"Every individual muon decays at exactly its mean lifetime and therefore travels an identical distance."}],"relativity-energy":[{"title":"Relativistic momentum and invariant mass","start":1,"theory":"Relativistic momentum is γmv using invariant rest mass. An increasing γ makes the momentum grow faster than classical mv at high speed. Avoid describing this as a change in the object's intrinsic mass; the course treatment keeps mass constant. The classical equation remains an approximation where v/c is small, not an alternative exact formula at all speeds.","question":"An object has mass 2.00 kg, speed 0.600c and γ = 1.25. Using c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, calculate relativistic momentum.","points":["Speed is 1.80 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹","p = γmv = 1.25 × 2.00 × 1.80 × 10⁸","p = 4.50 × 10⁸ kg m s⁻¹"],"claim":"The rest mass of an object increases when it moves faster relative to an observer."},{"title":"Rest, total and kinetic relativistic energy","start":3,"theory":"Rest energy mc² is part of total relativistic energy γmc². Kinetic energy is the excess over rest energy, so it is (γ − 1)mc². Confusing total and kinetic energy is especially serious near rest, where total energy remains substantial while kinetic energy tends to zero. Use joules or a consistently converted electronvolt energy unit throughout a calculation.","question":"A particle has rest energy 938 MeV and γ = 1.50. Find total and kinetic energies.","points":["Total energy is γErest = 1407 MeV","Kinetic energy is total minus rest energy","Kinetic energy is 469 MeV"],"claim":"A moving particle's kinetic energy equals its entire γmc² energy."},{"title":"Energy–momentum relations and cross-topic review","start":7,"theory":"The energy–momentum equation connects total energy, momentum and rest mass. It applies to particles beyond the low-speed regime. For a massless photon, it reduces to E = pc, while a massive particle retains the rest-energy term. General relativity addresses gravity and accelerated-frame contexts and should be distinguished from high-speed special-relativity momentum calculations.","question":"A photon has energy 6.00 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Find momentum using c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and explain why mv is unsuitable.","points":["For a photon E = pc","p = E/c = 2.00 × 10⁻²⁷ kg m s⁻¹","A photon has zero rest mass but nonzero momentum; classical mv is not its momentum model"],"claim":"A photon has zero momentum because its rest mass is zero."}],"general-relativity":[{"title":"Equivalence principle and curved spacetime","start":1,"theory":"A sufficiently small freely falling laboratory locally removes the apparent uniform gravitational force, motivating the equivalence principle. Tidal effects remain over larger regions and reveal spatial variation of gravity. Curved spacetime is a model relating matter and energy to motion; a rubber-sheet analogy is limited because it uses an external gravitational pull to illustrate gravity itself.","question":"Explain one similarity and one limitation when comparing a freely falling laboratory with a gravity-free inertial laboratory.","points":["An unrestrained nearby object can appear weightless in both local descriptions","Local measurements over a sufficiently small region can be equivalent","Tidal differences across a larger laboratory can reveal a nonuniform gravitational field"],"claim":"A freely falling observer proves gravity has vanished everywhere around the planet."},{"title":"Gravitational clock rates and GPS corrections","start":4,"theory":"A clock deeper in a gravitational field runs slower relative to a comparable clock higher in the field. Orbital motion also contributes special-relativistic clock effects. GPS requires both effects, with distinct physical origins. Clock rate is a comparison between worldlines and conditions, not a claim that the clock is mechanically faulty. Timing discrepancies become significant position errors at light speed.","question":"A navigation signal's timing error is 1.00 μs. Estimate the corresponding light-travel distance error with c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and identify two clock effects needing correction.","points":["Distance scale is cΔt = 300 m","Orbital motion contributes special-relativistic time dilation","Different gravitational conditions contribute general-relativistic clock-rate differences"],"claim":"GPS clock correction is needed only for satellite motion and never for gravitational effects."},{"title":"Black holes, lensing and gravitational-wave evidence","start":6,"theory":"A black hole's event horizon marks a causal boundary, not a material surface. Gravitational lensing follows light paths through curved spacetime and can create distorted or multiple images. Gravitational waves are propagating spacetime disturbances; they are not the everyday sound-like waves of a material medium. Distinguish observations from the theoretical interpretation they support.","question":"Explain how gravitational lensing and gravitational-wave detections provide different evidence about gravity.","points":["Lensing reveals deflection of light paths by mass-associated spacetime curvature","Gravitational-wave detections reveal propagating disturbances from changing massive systems","Both test consequences of general relativity through different observations"],"claim":"Gravitational waves require air or another material medium to travel through space."}],"electric-fields":[{"title":"Coulomb force and point-charge fields","start":1,"theory":"Coulomb's law gives force magnitude between charges; determine attraction or repulsion separately from signs. Electric field direction is defined by force on a positive test charge, so a negative charge experiences force opposite the field. Field strength is independent of the test charge in the undisturbed-field model. Convert microcoulombs carefully because the conversion appears in each charge factor.","question":"A −2.00 μC charge is in a uniform field of 3000 N C⁻¹ directed right. Find its force.","points":["q = −2.00 × 10⁻⁶ C","Force magnitude is charge magnitude × E = 0.00600 N","Force acts left because the charge is negative"],"claim":"Every charge experiences an electric force in the direction of the field."},{"title":"Superposition, field lines and potential","start":7,"theory":"Electric fields add as vectors, while potentials add as scalars. Zero field at a point does not imply zero potential there. Field lines run from positive toward negative charges and never cross. An equipotential has no potential difference along it, so moving a charge along it does no electrical work. Keep field strength in N C⁻¹ distinct from potential in volts.","question":"Two equal positive charges are equally distant on opposite sides of a midpoint. Explain the field at the midpoint and whether their potentials cancel.","points":["The fields have equal magnitudes and opposite directions","Their vector sum is zero at the midpoint","The positive scalar potentials add rather than cancel"],"claim":"Zero electric field at a point guarantees zero electric potential."},{"title":"Uniform plates, electrical work and particle acceleration","start":14,"theory":"Between ideal parallel plates, field magnitude is potential difference divided by separation. The field points from higher to lower electric potential, independent of which charge is later introduced. A negative charge accelerates opposite the field. Electrical potential-energy change is qΔV; kinetic-energy gain is its negative when other work is negligible. State signs or use a clearly identified energy-gain magnitude.","question":"An electron accelerates from rest through an accelerating potential difference of magnitude 200 V. Use e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C to calculate kinetic-energy gain.","points":["Kinetic-energy gain magnitude is eΔV","Gain is 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 200 = 3.20 × 10⁻¹⁷ J","This is 200 eV and comes from a decrease in electric potential energy"],"claim":"An electron always moves in the field-arrow direction between parallel plates."}],"magnetic-fields":[{"title":"Magnetic field geometry and current sources","start":1,"theory":"Use conventional current with a right-hand grip rule to determine a straight wire's field direction. Magnetic flux density B measures field strength in tesla; it is not magnetic flux through an area. Field diagrams use dots for out of the page and crosses for into the page. Around a wire, distance is measured from the wire's axis, and the ideal field weakens with increasing distance.","question":"Using B = μ₀I/(2πr), compare field magnitude at distances r and 3r from the same long straight wire.","points":["Current and μ₀ remain unchanged","The field is inversely proportional to distance r","At 3r the field is B/3"],"claim":"A long straight wire's magnetic field follows an inverse-square dependence on distance."},{"title":"Magnetic forces, circular particle paths and selectors","start":6,"theory":"A magnetic force is perpendicular to both velocity and field. It does no work on an ideal point charge, so it can change direction without changing speed. The perpendicular velocity component determines curved motion; parallel motion has no magnetic force. A velocity selector balances opposed electric and magnetic forces for one selected speed, independent of charge magnitude in the ideal equation.","question":"A charge crosses perpendicular electric and magnetic fields with E = 6000 V m⁻¹ and B = 0.0200 T. Find the speed for zero net force.","points":["Opposed force magnitudes satisfy qE = qvB","v = E/B","Selected speed is 3.00 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"],"claim":"A magnetic force accelerates an isolated charge along its velocity and increases its kinetic energy."},{"title":"Current-carrying conductors and parallel wires","start":13,"theory":"A conductor experiences magnetic force because its moving charges interact with the field and transfer force to the material. Use the length inside the field and the angle between current direction and B. For parallel currents, each wire's field acts on the other: same-direction currents attract and opposite-direction currents repel. Draw the field at the other wire before assigning force direction.","question":"A 0.300 m wire carries 4.00 A perpendicular to a 0.200 T field. Find force and explain the effect of making it parallel.","points":["F = BIL sinθ","Perpendicular force is 0.200 × 4.00 × 0.300 = 0.240 N","Parallel alignment gives sin0° = 0 and therefore zero magnetic force"],"claim":"A current-carrying wire feels the same magnetic force for every angle to the field."}],"motors":[{"title":"Motor forces and coil torque","start":1,"theory":"Opposite sides of a current loop can experience equal and opposite magnetic forces that produce a turning couple. Torque depends on coil area, turns, current, field and orientation. Identify whether the given angle is to the plane or to its normal before applying a sine or cosine factor. A zero resultant force does not prevent a nonzero torque.","question":"A 20-turn coil has area 0.0100 m², current 2.00 A and field 0.300 T. Find maximum torque.","points":["Maximum torque is NBIA","τmax = 20 × 0.300 × 2.00 × 0.0100","τmax = 0.120 N m"],"claim":"A pair of equal and opposite forces can never turn an object because their resultant force is zero."},{"title":"Commutators, orientation and maintaining rotation","start":5,"theory":"A DC motor's split-ring commutator reverses coil current each half-turn to maintain the intended torque direction. Brushes provide electrical contact while the coil rotates. Torque varies with orientation and vanishes momentarily at particular positions; inertia carries the coil through in the basic model. Slip rings maintain connections without reversing them and serve a different role in an AC generator.","question":"Explain why replacing a simple DC motor's split-ring commutator with continuous slip rings changes its operation.","points":["Without commutation the coil current does not reverse every half-turn","The magnetic torque reverses its tendency after the coil passes the relevant orientation","The simple arrangement no longer maintains the same-direction drive through successive half-turns"],"claim":"A motor's split-ring commutator has the same current-reversal function as continuous slip rings."},{"title":"Back emf, current and motor efficiency","start":11,"theory":"A rotating motor coil also acts as a generator. Its induced back emf opposes the supply-driven current. At startup rotation is small, so back emf is small and current can be large. Mechanical loading slows rotation, which can reduce back emf and increase current. Use energy accounting to separate useful mechanical output from heating and other losses.","question":"A motor on a 12.0 V supply has back emf 8.00 V and winding resistance 2.00 Ω. Find current and compare with the ideal stationary value.","points":["Current while rotating is (12.0 − 8.00)/2.00 = 2.00 A","At rest the stated ideal back emf is zero, so current is 12.0/2.00 = 6.00 A","Back emf reduces the net voltage driving current through the winding"],"claim":"A motor's back emf adds to the supply to increase the normal operating current."}],"induction":[{"title":"Flux, flux linkage and Faraday calculations","start":1,"theory":"Magnetic flux uses the field component normal to the area. Specify the angle between field and area normal; the angle to the plane uses a complementary trigonometric factor. Flux linkage multiplies flux by coil turns. Induced emf depends on how rapidly linkage changes, not just its instantaneous size. A stationary coil in a steady field can have flux without induced emf.","question":"A 50-turn coil's flux per turn falls from 0.00600 Wb to 0.00200 Wb in 0.100 s. Find average induced-emf magnitude.","points":["Flux change magnitude is 0.00400 Wb per turn","emf magnitude = N × flux-change magnitude/Δt","Magnitude is 50 × 0.00400/0.100 = 2.00 V"],"claim":"A coil with nonzero magnetic flux must always have a nonzero induced emf."},{"title":"Lenz's law and approaching or departing magnets","start":6,"theory":"Lenz's law opposes the change in flux, rather than simply opposing every existing field. Determine whether flux through the coil is increasing or decreasing, infer the induced field needed to oppose that change, then use a current-direction rule. This order prevents sign errors. The opposition is consistent with conservation of energy because mechanical work is required to maintain the motion.","question":"A north pole moves toward a closed conducting coil. Explain the coil face's induced polarity and the reason.","points":["The approaching magnet increases flux through the coil","The induced field opposes the increase","The near face behaves as a north pole and repels the approaching north pole"],"claim":"Lenz's law says the induced field always points opposite the existing field even when flux is decreasing."},{"title":"Motional emf, eddy currents and energy conservation","start":9,"theory":"A moving conductor crossing a magnetic field can separate charge through magnetic forces, producing a potential difference. In the perpendicular ideal geometry ε = Blv. A closed circuit carries current, and its magnetic force resists the motion. Eddy currents use the same induction principle in bulk conductors and can produce damping or heating. Their energy comes from external work or the driving field source.","question":"A 0.400 m rod moves at 3.00 m s⁻¹ perpendicular to a 0.500 T field. Find emf and identify the energy source if it drives a resistive circuit.","points":["ε = Blv = 0.500 × 0.400 × 3.00 = 0.600 V","An external agent must do work to maintain the motion against magnetic resistance","This mechanical input supplies the electrical energy and eventual heating"],"claim":"Induced electrical energy appears without any mechanical or electrical energy input."}],"generators":[{"title":"Rotating-coil flux and the AC waveform","start":1,"theory":"Rotation changes the angle between coil normal and field, causing sinusoidal flux in the ideal model. Emf is related to the rate of flux change, so maximum flux and maximum emf occur at different orientations. Increasing turns, area, field or angular speed can increase peak emf. Keep coil angle and time-phase conventions explicit rather than memorising a diagram without its definitions.","question":"For an ideal rotating coil with N = 100, B = 0.200 T, A = 0.0100 m² and ω = 50.0 rad s⁻¹, find peak emf.","points":["εmax = NBAω","εmax = 100 × 0.200 × 0.0100 × 50.0","Peak emf is 10.0 V"],"claim":"An ideal generator's emf is greatest exactly when the magnetic flux through its coil is greatest."},{"title":"Frequency, slip rings and energy transformation","start":5,"theory":"Mechanical rotation supplies a generator's energy. Slip rings and brushes preserve connection to the rotating coil, allowing an alternating output in a simple AC generator. A DC generator's commutator changes the output connection each half-turn. Frequency is linked to rotation rate and pole arrangement; use the stated simple model rather than assuming every generator produces one cycle per turn.","question":"A simple two-pole generator rotates at 3000 revolutions per minute and produces one electrical cycle per revolution. Find frequency, period and angular speed.","points":["Frequency is 3000/60 = 50.0 Hz","Period is 1/50.0 = 0.0200 s","Angular speed is 2πf = 314 rad s⁻¹"],"claim":"A generator supplies electrical energy without taking mechanical energy from its driving source."},{"title":"RMS voltage and resistive AC power","start":9,"theory":"The mean voltage of a symmetric sinusoid is zero, but heating is not zero because power depends on voltage squared. RMS values give equivalent resistive heating compared with DC. For a sinusoid, peak and RMS differ by √2. Use RMS voltage and current in average resistive-power equations, not peak values unless the equation explicitly includes their averaging factor.","question":"A sinusoidal supply has peak voltage 100 V across a 50.0 Ω resistor. Find RMS voltage and average power.","points":["Vrms = 100/√2 = 70.7 V","Pavg = Vrms²/R","Average power is 100 W"],"claim":"An AC supply with zero mean voltage delivers zero average heating power to a resistor."}],"transformers":[{"title":"Mutual induction and transformer voltage ratios","start":1,"theory":"A transformer transfers energy through changing magnetic flux linking two coils. It requires an alternating or otherwise changing input; steady DC does not sustain the normal secondary emf. In the ideal ratio Vsecondary/Vprimary = Nsecondary/Nprimary, voltage increases with turns. The coils need not be electrically connected to each other because their coupling is magnetic.","question":"An ideal transformer has 400 primary turns and 100 secondary turns on a 240 V AC input. Find output voltage and explain whether steady DC gives the same output.","points":["Vs/Vp = Ns/Np = 100/400","Secondary voltage is 60.0 V AC","Steady DC does not maintain changing flux and therefore does not give the normal sustained secondary voltage"],"claim":"A transformer gives its normal turns-ratio output continuously from a steady DC supply."},{"title":"Power conservation and high-voltage transmission","start":4,"theory":"Ideal input and output powers are equal, so stepping up voltage steps down current. This does not increase energy or create power. For fixed transmitted power, increasing line voltage reduces line current and resistive loss I²R. Distinguish transformer current ratios from the separate voltage drop along a transmission line. Always use the total relevant line resistance.","question":"An ideal transformer raises 100 V at 5.00 A to 1000 V. Find output current and compare line loss with the input-voltage case for the same resistance.","points":["Transferred power is 100 × 5.00 = 500 W","Output current is 500/1000 = 0.500 A","The current is ten times smaller, so line loss is one hundredth for the same line resistance"],"claim":"A step-up transformer increases both voltage and current while conserving power."},{"title":"Real losses, efficiency and power-grid reasoning","start":10,"theory":"Real transformers lose energy through winding resistance, eddy currents and hysteresis. Laminated cores reduce large eddy-current paths; suitable core materials limit magnetic-cycle losses. Efficiency is output power divided by input power. Generation, high-voltage transmission and lower-voltage distribution serve distinct functions. A real system's lower losses improve efficiency without eliminating every transfer to the surroundings.","question":"A transformer receives 800 W and delivers 720 W. Find efficiency and total loss, and name a way to reduce core eddy-current heating.","points":["Efficiency is 720/800 × 100% = 90.0%","Loss is 800 − 720 = 80 W","Laminating the core interrupts large eddy-current paths"],"claim":"A transformer with 90% efficiency loses 90% of its input power."}],"quantum":[{"title":"Photons, electromagnetic waves and quantised energy","start":1,"theory":"Light propagation has wave features, while energy exchange with matter occurs in photons. Photon energy grows with frequency and decreases with wavelength. Intensity can be increased by increasing photon arrival rate without changing each photon's energy. Convert nanometres to metres before using E = hc/λ. The photon model complements rather than erases evidence for diffraction and interference.","question":"Find photon energy for wavelength 600 nm using h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹. Explain a doubling of intensity at this wavelength.","points":["600 nm = 6.00 × 10⁻⁷ m","E = hc/λ = 3.32 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","Doubling intensity at unchanged wavelength increases photon arrival rate rather than photon energy"],"claim":"Doubling monochromatic light intensity necessarily doubles the energy of each photon."},{"title":"Photoelectric thresholds and stopping potential","start":4,"theory":"A photon must supply at least the work function to release an electron. Above threshold, excess photon energy becomes maximum electron kinetic energy. Increasing intensity increases emission rate when emission is possible, but does not raise the maximum kinetic energy at fixed frequency. Stopping potential measures that maximum through eVs, while collected current probes the emission rate.","question":"Light with photon energy 4.50 eV strikes a surface of work function 2.00 eV. Find maximum electron kinetic energy and stopping potential.","points":["Kmax = Ephoton − φ = 2.50 eV","eVs = Kmax","Stopping-potential magnitude is 2.50 V"],"claim":"Increasing light intensity below threshold must eventually eject electrons in the simple one-photon photoelectric model."},{"title":"Atomic spectra, matter waves and complementary models","start":8,"theory":"Bound atomic states have discrete energies. Emission occurs when an atom transitions to a lower state, with photon energy equal to the state-energy difference. Absorption requires a matching allowed transition. Matter wavelength relates to momentum through λ = h/p, not to a particle's macroscopic diameter. Wave and particle models are selected by the observation being explained.","question":"An electron transition releases a 2.00 eV photon. Find wavelength using hc = 1240 eV nm and identify the transition direction.","points":["λ = hc/E = 1240/2.00 = 620 nm","The atom changes from a higher to a lower energy state","The discrete state-energy difference fixes the emitted wavelength"],"claim":"An emission photon appears when the atom moves to a higher energy level."}],"particle-physics":[{"title":"Electric acceleration and magnetic steering","start":1,"theory":"Electric forces can do work and increase particle speed. Magnetic forces can steer a moving charge without adding kinetic energy in the ideal static-field model. A circular magnetic path needs inward force balanced by qvB, with the appropriate momentum treatment at high speed. Keep accelerating electric fields distinct from steering magnetic fields when explaining an accelerator.","question":"A proton gains energy through an accelerating potential magnitude of 5.00 kV. Using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, find its energy gain and explain a magnetic steering section.","points":["Energy gain is eV = 8.00 × 10⁻¹⁶ J, or 5.00 keV","An electric field supplies the kinetic-energy gain","A magnetic steering field changes direction without doing work on the ideal charge"],"claim":"Magnetic steering increases particle kinetic energy in the same way as an accelerating electric field."},{"title":"Linacs, cyclotrons and synchrotrons","start":4,"theory":"A linac uses electric acceleration along a sequence of stages. A basic cyclotron alternates acceleration between semicircular magnetic paths, relying on an approximately constant nonrelativistic orbital frequency. At high energy relativity changes the timing and momentum relationships. A synchrotron adjusts fields and acceleration timing to maintain a selected orbit while momentum increases.","question":"Explain why a simple nonrelativistic cyclotron's fixed acceleration timing becomes inadequate at very high particle speed.","points":["Relativistic momentum is larger than mv","The orbital timing no longer follows the unchanged simple nonrelativistic assumption","Acceleration timing and/or magnetic field must be adjusted to remain synchronised"],"claim":"A basic fixed-frequency cyclotron's nonrelativistic timing remains exact arbitrarily close to light speed."},{"title":"Relativistic beam energy and collision evidence","start":7,"theory":"Particle collisions conserve total energy and momentum. Available collision energy can create particles if all relevant conservation constraints are satisfied. Opposing beams can provide more centre-of-momentum energy than a comparable beam striking a stationary target because less energy remains in bulk motion. Detectors infer particle properties from tracks, energy deposits and timing rather than directly seeing tiny objects.","question":"A beam particle has rest energy 500 MeV and total energy 1500 MeV. Find γ and kinetic energy.","points":["γ = Etotal/Erest = 3.00","Kinetic energy is Etotal − Erest","Kinetic energy is 1000 MeV"],"claim":"All the total energy of a moving massive particle is available as its kinetic energy."}],"cosmology":[{"title":"Astronomical distances and spectral redshift","start":1,"theory":"An astronomical unit describes a Solar System distance scale, while light-year and parsec describe much larger distances. A light-year is distance, not time. Redshift compares measured spectral wavelengths with known rest wavelengths. Identify the same spectral feature before using the ratio. Low-redshift speed approximations must not be extended to arbitrary cosmological redshifts without an appropriate expansion model.","question":"A known spectral line at 500 nm is observed at 510 nm. Calculate redshift and estimate recession speed using v ≈ zc with c = 3.00 × 10⁵ km s⁻¹.","points":["z = (510 − 500)/500 = 0.0200","Using the stated low-redshift approximation, v ≈ 0.0200c","Estimated speed is 6000 km s⁻¹"],"claim":"A light-year measures how old a star is rather than a distance."},{"title":"Hubble's law and expansion of space","start":5,"theory":"Hubble's relation connects recession speed and distance for the applicable scale and approximation. Its gradient has units of speed divided by distance, not acceleration. Expansion is described by changing large-scale separations, not by galaxies exploding from a special centre through pre-existing space. Gravitationally bound local systems do not simply expand according to the same distant-galaxy relation.","question":"With H₀ = 70.0 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹, estimate recession speed at 100 Mpc and explain the Hubble-graph gradient.","points":["v = H₀d = 7000 km s⁻¹","On a speed-versus-distance graph the gradient is H₀","Its units are km s⁻¹ per Mpc, not acceleration units"],"claim":"Hubble's law identifies our galaxy as the unique centre of the universe's expansion."},{"title":"Big Bang evidence and the early-universe sequence","start":9,"theory":"A strong model explains several independent observations. Expansion and redshift, the near-thermal cosmic microwave background and light-element abundances support a hot dense early universe. Recombination allowed photons to travel more freely when neutral atoms formed; it is distinct from the much earlier synthesis of light nuclei. Compare each observation with the specific prediction it tests rather than listing evidence without explanation.","question":"Link the cosmic microwave background and primordial light-element abundance to the hot Big Bang model.","points":["The CMB is relic radiation from an earlier hot universe that cooled as space expanded","Light-element abundances constrain early-universe nuclear processes","Independent observations test different predictions of the same evolving-universe model"],"claim":"The cosmic microwave background was first created by modern stars in nearby galaxies."}]},"extras":[{"id":"relativistic-velocity-addition","parent":"relativity-foundations","title":"Relativistic velocity addition","theory":["Choose the laboratory as frame S and the moving spacecraft as S′. In one dimension, if S′ moves at v relative to S and the object moves at u′ relative to S′, its laboratory velocity is u = (u′ + v)/(1 + u′v/c²). The signs encode direction. This relationship replaces simple addition at high speed and reduces to that approximation when the correction term is very small.","A spacecraft moving at 0.600c launches an object forward at 0.500c in the spacecraft frame. Classical addition predicts 1.100c, exposing the failure of the approximation. The relativistic denominator is 1.300 and the laboratory result is 0.846c. If the spacecraft emits forward light instead, substituting u′ = c gives u = c, consistent with the invariant vacuum light speed.","For the inverse transformation use u′ = (u − v)/(1 − uv/c²). Name the observer for every reported velocity and keep all quantities in matching units. The equation is for collinear velocities; do not apply it componentwise without the appropriate multidimensional transformation."],"model":{"q":"A spacecraft travels at 0.600c and ejects a probe backward at 0.400c in its own frame. Find the probe's laboratory velocity.","steps":["With forward positive, v = +0.600c and u′ = −0.400c.","The numerator is (+0.600 − 0.400)c = 0.200c.","The denominator is 1 + (−0.400)(0.600) = 0.760.","u = 0.200c/0.760 = +0.263c: the probe still travels forward in the laboratory."]},"guided":{"q":"A spacecraft moves at 0.500c and a forward probe moves at 0.500c relative to it. Predict whether the laboratory speed reaches c, then calculate it.","steps":["Take v = u′ = +0.500c.","Numerator is 1.000c; denominator is 1.250.","Laboratory speed is 0.800c, below c."]},"exam":{"q":"A spacecraft at 0.800c emits light backward. Determine its laboratory velocity using relativistic velocity addition and interpret the result.","points":["Use v = +0.800c and u′ = −c.","u = (−1 + 0.800)c/(1 − 0.800) = −c.","The light travels backward at vacuum speed c in the laboratory too."]},"claim":"Light emitted backward by a moving spacecraft travels more slowly than c in the laboratory.","diagram":"frames"},{"id":"young-double-slit","parent":"quantum","title":"Young's double slit and single-particle interference","theory":["Two coherent slits produce waves with a stable relative phase. At a screen point their path difference determines constructive or destructive interference. For narrow slits and a distant screen, in-phase slits have bright fringes where path difference is nλ. The central maximum corresponds to equal path lengths.","In the small-angle geometry, adjacent bright-fringe spacing is Δy = λL/d, where L is slit-to-screen distance and d is slit separation. Increasing wavelength or screen distance increases the spacing; increasing slit separation decreases it. The formula is a geometric approximation and its symbols must not be swapped.","Individual photons or electrons arrive as localised detections, but many detections accumulate into an interference pattern when coherence is retained. This is evidence for wave–particle duality. Determining which slit each particle uses changes the experimental conditions and generally removes the interference pattern."],"model":{"q":"Light of wavelength 600 nm passes through slits separated by 0.300 mm onto a screen 2.00 m away. Find fringe spacing.","steps":["Convert λ = 6.00 × 10⁻⁷ m and d = 3.00 × 10⁻⁴ m.","Use Δy = λL/d in the small-angle model.","Δy = (6.00 × 10⁻⁷)(2.00)/(3.00 × 10⁻⁴).","Spacing is 0.00400 m, or 4.00 mm."]},"guided":{"q":"Keep the wavelength and slit separation unchanged but move that screen to 1.00 m. Find the spacing.","steps":["Fringe spacing is directly proportional to L.","The screen distance is halved.","The spacing is therefore 2.00 mm."]},"exam":{"q":"In the same small-angle setup, the slit separation doubles while wavelength and screen distance stay fixed. Predict the fringe spacing change and explain what single-photon detections show.","points":["Δy is inversely proportional to slit separation, so spacing halves.","Each photon gives a localised detection.","Many detections build the interference pattern, demonstrating wave-like statistical behaviour."]},"claim":"A single-photon experiment cannot produce an interference pattern after many detections.","diagram":"slits"},{"id":"light-polarisation","parent":"quantum","title":"Polarisation and the transverse nature of light","theory":["An electromagnetic wave has electric and magnetic field oscillations perpendicular to each other and to propagation. Polarisation specifies the electric-field oscillation direction or pattern. A simple linear polariser transmits one field component, demonstrating a transverse wave property. A purely longitudinal sound wave has no equivalent transverse oscillation direction to select.","For ideal linearly polarised light, a second polariser at angle θ to the first transmits intensity I = I₀cos²θ. This quantitative relation is used here as an extension model. Parallel axes maximise transmission; crossed axes give zero in the ideal model. Distinguish the field-amplitude projection from its squared intensity relationship.","Real filters are imperfect and absorb energy. For ideal unpolarised incident light, the first polariser transmits half the intensity before any second-filter calculation. Do not apply that half factor again when the incoming beam is already aligned linearly polarised light."],"model":{"q":"Already polarised light of intensity 80.0 W m⁻² passes through an ideal analyser 60.0° from its polarisation axis. Find transmitted intensity.","steps":["The light is already polarised, so no initial half-intensity factor is needed.","Use the stated extension model I = I₀cos²θ.","cos²60.0° = 0.250.","I = 80.0 × 0.250 = 20.0 W m⁻²."]},"guided":{"q":"The analyser turns to 45.0°. What intensity is transmitted from that 80.0 W m⁻² already polarised beam?","steps":["cos²45.0° = 0.500.","I = 80.0 × 0.500.","The transmitted intensity is 40.0 W m⁻²."]},"exam":{"q":"Explain why ideal crossed polarisers block linearly polarised light and why this supports a transverse model.","points":["The second transmission axis is perpendicular to the first polarisation direction.","The electric field has no component along the second axis in the ideal model.","Selecting an oscillation direction perpendicular to travel is a transverse-wave property."]},"claim":"Ordinary longitudinal sound in air can be polarised with the same transverse-axis mechanism as light.","diagram":"polarisation"},{"id":"black-body-radiation","parent":"quantum","title":"Black-body spectra and the origin of quantum theory","theory":["An ideal black body absorbs all incident radiation and emits a temperature-dependent continuous spectrum. As temperature rises, the spectrum's peak shifts to shorter wavelength and its total emitted power increases. A black body need not look black when hot: its visible emission can be bright. The peak wavelength is not the only wavelength emitted.","Classical equipartition predictions failed at short wavelengths, producing the ultraviolet catastrophe. Planck introduced discrete oscillator energies to explain the measured spectral shape. Quantum theory emerged to address a specific experimental failure, rather than because wave phenomena disappeared.","As an extension calculation, Wien's displacement relationship λmaxT = b uses absolute temperature and b = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K. A wavelength-form spectrum and a frequency-form spectrum have different peak locations, so use the stated representation. Real objects can depart from ideal black-body behaviour through wavelength-dependent emissivity."],"model":{"q":"Estimate the peak wavelength of a 5800 K ideal black body using the stated Wien extension model.","steps":["Use λmax = b/T.","λmax = (2.90 × 10⁻³)/5800 m.","The result is 5.00 × 10⁻⁷ m.","The wavelength peak is 500 nm, while the object emits a broad spectrum."]},"guided":{"q":"If the temperature doubles in this model, what happens to the wavelength peak?","steps":["λmax is inversely proportional to T.","Doubling T halves λmax.","The peak shifts to shorter wavelength; it is not the whole spectrum's single wavelength."]},"exam":{"q":"Describe two changes to a black-body spectrum when temperature rises, and state what the ultraviolet catastrophe challenged.","points":["The wavelength peak moves to shorter wavelength.","The total emitted power increases.","The observed short-wavelength spectrum contradicted the classical prediction and motivated quantised-energy modelling."]},"claim":"A black body emits radiation at only its peak wavelength.","diagram":"spectrum"},{"id":"photoelectric-planck-investigation","parent":"quantum","title":"Determining Planck's constant from photoelectric data","theory":["The photoelectric energy equation Kmax = hf − φ predicts a linear relationship between maximum electron kinetic energy and incident frequency. A stopping potential measures Kmax through eVs, so Vs = (h/e)f − φ/e. The graph's slope is h/e, not h, when its vertical axis is voltage.","Use a spread of above-threshold frequencies and determine a best-fit slope from widely separated points on the fitted line. The horizontal intercept estimates threshold frequency and the vertical intercept gives −φ/e. An intercept is an extrapolated model quantity; the source does not produce negative photoelectron kinetic energies below threshold.","Control the surface and measurement conditions, convert frequency-axis scale factors correctly and distinguish intensity from frequency. Repeating measurements can reduce random uncertainty, while a voltmeter offset may systematically shift the intercept. A straight-line fit does not justify ignoring anomalous points without investigating their cause."],"model":{"q":"A Vs-versus-f best-fit gradient is 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ V s. Estimate h using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.","steps":["The graph equation gives gradient = h/e.","Rearrange h = e × gradient.","h = (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹)(4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵).","h = 6.62 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, using C V = J."]},"guided":{"q":"The graph's voltage intercept is −2.00 V. Find the work function in eV and joules using e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.","steps":["Intercept = −φ/e, so φ/e = 2.00 V.","Work function is 2.00 eV.","In joules, φ = 3.20 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."]},"exam":{"q":"On a Kmax-versus-frequency graph with energy in joules, identify the slope and intercept and explain why intensity does not set the slope.","points":["The slope is h.","The vertical intercept is −φ.","Intensity changes photon arrival rate; the energy-per-photon relation hf determines the frequency dependence."]},"claim":"The gradient of every photoelectric graph is Planck's constant regardless of the axes.","diagram":"photoelectric"},{"id":"fluorescence-phosphorescence","parent":"quantum","title":"Fluorescence, phosphorescence and energy levels","theory":["An absorbed photon can excite an atom or molecule into a higher energy state. Some energy may be transferred non-radiatively before an emitted photon is produced. The emitted photon can therefore have lower energy and longer wavelength than the absorbed photon. Energy is conserved across both radiative and non-radiative transfers.","Fluorescence is prompt emission following excitation and typically ends rapidly when the excitation source is removed. Phosphorescence involves longer-lived states and can persist after the source is removed. Persistence does not mean energy is being created: stored excitation energy is released over time.","Applications include fluorescent markers, forensic visualisation and glow materials. A useful explanation connects the excitation wavelength, allowed energy changes and emission behaviour. The simple level diagram is a model; real molecular spectra contain additional vibrational and environmental structure."],"model":{"q":"A material absorbs a 4.00 eV photon, transfers 1.00 eV non-radiatively and emits one photon. Find the emitted energy and wavelength using hc = 1240 eV nm.","steps":["Account for all energy transfers.","Emitted photon energy is 4.00 − 1.00 = 3.00 eV.","λ = hc/E = 1240/3.00.","Emitted wavelength is 413 nm, longer than the 310 nm absorbed wavelength."]},"guided":{"q":"If the absorbed energy remains 4.00 eV but 2.00 eV is transferred non-radiatively, find the emitted wavelength.","steps":["The emitted photon has energy 2.00 eV.","λ = 1240/2.00.","The emitted wavelength is 620 nm."]},"exam":{"q":"Explain why a glow material can emit after its lamp is switched off without violating conservation of energy, and distinguish phosphorescence from prompt fluorescence.","points":["Excitation energy was stored while the material was illuminated.","Phosphorescence releases energy through longer-lived states after illumination ends.","Prompt fluorescence normally ceases rapidly when excitation is removed; neither process creates energy."]},"claim":"A phosphorescent material creates new energy because it glows after the external light is removed.","diagram":"levels"},{"id":"x-ray-production","parent":"quantum","title":"Continuous and characteristic X-ray production","theory":["Electrons accelerated through a potential difference gain kinetic energy. When they strike a target, deceleration can emit a continuous bremsstrahlung spectrum. The maximum photon energy cannot exceed one incoming electron's available kinetic energy; this sets a minimum wavelength. Much of the input energy is transferred as heat rather than X-rays.","Characteristic X-rays arise when inner electron-shell vacancies are filled by electrons from higher energy states. Their photon energies are discrete and depend on the target's atomic energy structure. Raising tube voltage changes the continuous spectrum's energy limit and may enable characteristic emission, but does not continuously shift a fixed target's characteristic line energies.","Imaging and material-analysis applications depend on penetration and interactions with matter. Contrast, detector response and exposure are part of interpreting an image. Keep characteristic lines, the continuous background and the wavelength cutoff distinct when reading a spectrum."],"model":{"q":"An X-ray tube accelerates electrons through 40.0 kV. Find maximum photon energy and minimum wavelength using hc = 1240 eV nm.","steps":["Maximum electron kinetic energy is eV = 40000 eV.","The maximum possible single-photon energy is 40.0 keV.","Minimum wavelength is hc/Emax = 1240/40000 nm.","λmin = 0.0310 nm."]},"guided":{"q":"The tube voltage rises to 80.0 kV. Predict the new minimum wavelength.","steps":["The maximum photon energy doubles.","λmin is inversely proportional to that energy.","The minimum wavelength halves to 0.0155 nm."]},"exam":{"q":"Distinguish the continuous X-ray spectrum from characteristic X-ray lines, including the origin of each.","points":["The continuous spectrum comes from varying energy transfers during electron deceleration.","Characteristic lines come from discrete atomic electron transitions after inner-shell vacancies form.","Their line energies depend on the target's energy levels rather than simply matching every possible tube voltage."]},"claim":"All X-rays from a tube have the same energy equal to the full electron accelerating energy.","diagram":"xray"},{"id":"laser-stimulated-emission","parent":"quantum","title":"Stimulated emission and the laser","theory":["Absorption raises an atom to an allowed higher state. Spontaneous emission releases a photon without a triggering incident photon. Stimulated emission occurs when an appropriate photon induces a matching transition, producing another photon with the same frequency, direction and phase in the ideal description. These mechanisms must be distinguished to explain amplification.","A laser requires an energy pump and a population inversion between the relevant states. A resonant optical cavity selects and reinforces suitable light, with a partially transmitting mirror allowing output. The pump provides the energy; the trigger photon does not independently power an unlimited chain of emissions.","Laser light can have narrow spectral width and high coherence, but these features do not imply that every laser has identical wavelength or power. Application answers should connect the desired beam property to the task, such as precise alignment, reading information or concentrating energy."],"model":{"q":"A laser transition has energy difference 1.96 eV. Find wavelength using hc = 1240 eV nm and identify the energy source.","steps":["The emitted photon energy equals the level difference.","λ = hc/ΔE = 1240/1.96 nm.","The wavelength is approximately 633 nm.","The pumping system replenishes the excited-state energy used in emission."]},"guided":{"q":"A transition's energy difference doubles. Predict what happens to emitted photon frequency and wavelength.","steps":["E = hf makes frequency proportional to transition energy.","Frequency doubles.","Since c = fλ, wavelength halves."]},"exam":{"q":"Explain how stimulated emission and population inversion enable optical amplification without creating energy.","points":["An incident matching photon triggers an excited-state transition producing a matching photon.","Population inversion makes stimulated emission exceed absorption for the relevant transition.","A pump supplies and replenishes the stored excitation energy."]},"claim":"A laser amplifies light without requiring an external energy input.","diagram":"laser"},{"id":"photovoltaic-cells","parent":"quantum","title":"Photovoltaic cells and photon-to-electrical energy transfer","theory":["A photovoltaic device absorbs suitable photons to create mobile charge carriers in a semiconductor. An internal junction field separates carriers, allowing electrical energy to be delivered to an external circuit. The energy originates in incident radiation. A photon below the relevant absorption threshold generally cannot create the needed carrier excitation in the simple one-photon model.","Increasing suitable illumination can increase generated current, but output also depends on voltage, temperature, device properties and load. Not all incident energy becomes useful electrical output: reflection, incomplete absorption and thermalisation contribute to losses. Use output power VI and compare it with radiant power incident on the active area.","The simplified threshold is linked to the semiconductor band gap, not a metal photoelectric work function. Both involve quantum energy conditions, but the mechanisms and device structures differ. Treat a cell as an energy converter rather than a store that emits previously accumulated sunlight indefinitely."],"model":{"q":"A cell of area 0.0200 m² receives irradiance 800 W m⁻² and delivers 0.500 V at 4.00 A. Find efficiency.","steps":["Incident power is irradiance × area = 800 × 0.0200 = 16.0 W.","Useful electrical power is VI = 0.500 × 4.00 = 2.00 W.","Efficiency is Pout/Pin × 100%.","Efficiency is 12.5%."]},"guided":{"q":"Under the same input, output current falls to 3.00 A while voltage stays 0.500 V. Find the efficiency.","steps":["Output power is 1.50 W.","Incident power remains 16.0 W.","Efficiency is 1.50/16.0 × 100% = 9.38%."]},"exam":{"q":"Explain why photons below a semiconductor's relevant excitation threshold cannot be assumed to produce normal photocurrent merely by increasing their number.","points":["Each photon supplies a discrete energy hf.","A single suitable carrier excitation requires sufficient energy to meet the relevant threshold.","Increasing photon number raises intensity but not each photon's energy in this one-photon model."]},"claim":"Increasing the number of below-threshold photons necessarily increases each photon's energy.","diagram":"photovoltaic"},{"id":"led-quantum-emission","parent":"quantum","title":"LED photon energy, colour and circuit design","theory":["An LED emits light when carriers recombine across semiconductor energy states. The photon energy is related to the material's relevant energy separation, so different semiconductor compositions can give different colours. The energy comes from the electrical supply. An LED needs appropriate current limitation and correct bias; its colour is not selected simply by applying any desired supply voltage.","Use E = hf = hc/λ to connect colour with photon energy. A simple energy-gap estimate gives the emitted wavelength, but a real LED has a spectral width and additional electrical losses. Distinguish photon energy, junction voltage and the total supply voltage shared with other components.","A series resistor limits current by taking the voltage remaining after the diode's forward drop. Efficiency compares optical output with electrical input and is not generally 100%. This lesson connects Year 11 circuit modelling with Year 12 discrete energy transitions."],"model":{"q":"An idealised LED emits photons of energy 2.00 eV. Estimate wavelength using hc = 1240 eV nm, then find a series resistor for a 5.00 V supply, 2.00 V forward drop and 0.0200 A.","steps":["λ = 1240/2.00 = 620 nm.","The resistor takes 5.00 − 2.00 = 3.00 V.","R = 3.00/0.0200.","The current-limiting resistance is 150 Ω in the stated operating model."]},"guided":{"q":"An LED's photon energy is 3.10 eV. Find its approximate wavelength and compare its colour's photon energy with the 2.00 eV model.","steps":["λ = 1240/3.10 = 400 nm.","The wavelength is shorter than 620 nm.","Its emitted photons have higher energy and higher frequency."]},"exam":{"q":"Explain why using a higher supply voltage does not simply set any desired LED colour, and identify the function of its series resistor.","points":["Photon energy is linked to the semiconductor's relevant energy-state separation.","Changing supply voltage mainly changes the operating current and voltage distribution, not an arbitrary selectable energy gap.","A correctly chosen series resistor limits current by taking the remaining supply voltage."]},"claim":"An LED's colour can always be chosen just by turning its supply voltage up or down.","diagram":"led"}]};
